export const OPENERS_A = {
  "fish/african-cichlid": [
    "An African cichlid, on this page, means a rift-lake fish from Malawi, Tanganyika, or Victoria. It does not mean an oscar, an angelfish, or any other South American cichlid that shares the family name. Adult size runs from about 3 inches in a small mbuna to 12 inches in a large hap or Tanganyikan predator, and the lifespan line is 8 to 15 years. That range is why one bag labeled assorted African cichlid so often becomes a fight. The fish in the bag are not one species with one adult size.",
    "The water is hard and alkaline. Hold the tank at 76 to 82°F, pH 7.8 to 8.6, general hardness 12 to 20 dGH, and carbonate hardness 10 to 18 dKH. Soft, acidic water that keeps a cardinal tetra happy will leave these fish washed out and stressed. A 55-gallon tank is the minimum on this page. It is already small once several mbuna start claiming caves. Ammonia and nitrite stay at 0 ppm. Nitrate belongs under 20 ppm, which means the filter has to match the bioload of fish that dig and eat all day.",
    "Mbuna are the rock dwellers. They graze aufwuchs, so the diet leans herbivore even though the group as a whole is listed as omnivore or herbivore depending on species. A pile of holey rock, and more than one cave per fish, is the layout. Haps and utaka want open sand and a longer swim. Tanganyikan shell-dwellers live in snail shells on sand and can be kept on a different plan than a mbuna pile, but they still want the same hard alkaline water. Mixing those strategies in one tank is how a shell-dweller ends up homeless.",
    "Temperament runs from semi-aggressive to aggressive. One male of a given mbuna species, several females, and enough rock to break lines of sight is the usual pattern. Two males of the same species in a 55-gallon tank usually means one fish pinned in a corner. Overstocking a long Malawi tank can spread the chasing out, but only when the filter keeps up. Adding tetras, angelfish, or rams to calm the tank puts those fish in water they cannot live in, and the cichlids will harass them anyway.",
    "Victoria cichlids are the third lake, not a color morph of a yellow lab. Many of the fish sold from that lake are captive lines of species that are rare or gone in the wild, and they still need the hard water and the space. Buy a named fish when the shop can name it. A juvenile that looks like a 4-inch neighbor may be a species that doubles that length and changes the whole stocking. Eight to 15 years is long enough for the largest fish in the bag to become the only one left.",
  ],

  "fish/amano-shrimp": [
    "Amano shrimp are Caridina multidentata, the algae shrimp popularized by Takashi Amano. Adults reach about 2 inches and live 2 to 3 years. Wild animals come from Japan, Taiwan, and Korea. They are not cherry shrimp, and they are not the clear feeder shrimp sold as ghosts. A planted tank of at least 10 gallons, already running long enough to grow a little algae and biofilm, is the setup they actually work in. A sterile new tank has nothing for them to eat.",
    "Keep the adults in freshwater. The table range is 70 to 78°F, pH 6.5 to 7.5, general hardness 6 to 8 dGH, and carbonate hardness 3 to 6 dKH. Nitrate under 20 ppm, with ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm, matters more than hitting a single perfect pH. They do well with small peaceful fish. A cichlid large enough to inhale a 2-inch shrimp will do exactly that. Copper medications sold for fish parasites kill Caridina. If a tankmate needs copper, the shrimp have to leave first.",
    "They are freshwater algae eaters. Hair algae and the film on wood and older leaves are the foods they are bought for. They will not erase a tank that is already choked with black beard algae in a weekend, and they will starve in a spotless tank where nobody feeds them. A sinking pellet or a slice of blanched zucchini, offered after the lights go out, is the backup ration when the glass is already clean. They are poor at competing with fast fish for flakes that never reach the bottom.",
    "Breeding is the claim that goes wrong in freshwater. Females will carry eggs. The larvae that hatch need salt water to develop. They do not grow up in the display tank. The care card marks breeding very difficult for that reason. A keeper who wants babies that survive in the same water as the adults should be looking at Neocaridina, not at an Amano female with a dark saddle of eggs. She can drop a cloud of larvae and the adult count will not change.",
    "Buy a group. One shrimp in a 10-gallon tank sits on one leaf and does little visible grazing. A half dozen will work the moss, the wood, and the back glass. A clear shell on the sand is a molt, not a corpse, if the same number of eyed shrimp is still walking around. A whole group dead after a water change is a parameter swing or a copper residue, not bad luck. The adult animal is listed as easy. Raising the larvae is the part that is not.",
  ],

  "fish/angelfish": [
    "Freshwater angelfish are Pterophyllum scalare, a tall Amazon cichlid. The body grows to about 6 inches long. The dorsal and anal fins make the fish 8 to 10 inches tall. That height is why the minimum is a 30-gallon tall tank, not a long shallow tank with the same gallon count. A fish that cannot turn without folding its fins into the gravel is in the wrong shape of water. Lifespan is 10 to 12 years when the water stays clean and the fins stay intact.",
    "Hold them at 76 to 84°F, pH 6.0 to 7.5, and hardness around 3 to 8 dGH. That is softer water than a livebearer tank and the wrong water for a Lake Malawi setup. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm and nitrate under 20 ppm are the standing limits. They are omnivores. A cichlid pellet they can bite, plus frozen bloodworms or brine shrimp, matches the way they feed in the middle of the water column. Flakes that sink past them before they notice are a poor staple for a fish this tall.",
    "Temperament is semi-aggressive, and a bonded pair is the moment that label becomes obvious. Angels clean a broad leaf or a piece of slate and then own that corner. Tetras that were ignored for months become food or get driven into the heater. Juveniles can be grown as a group in a tall tank. Once a pair forms, the spare angels usually have to move. Six adults in the 30-gallon minimum is not a stocking plan. The 30 is the smallest tank that fits the fin span of one or two fish.",
    "Fin-nippers and angelfish do not share a tank. Tiger barbs will reduce a veil-fin angel to stumps, and the angel stops eating once the damage is bad. Very small fish, including newborn livebearers and tiny rasboras, fit in an adult mouth. A school of cardinal tetras is the classic dither, and even those are safer in a tank larger than the minimum. Slow fish with long fins, such as fancy guppies, get the worst of the sparring even when nobody is spawning.",
    "Most shop fish are scalare, or a farmed line of it. Altum angels are a different species, taller still, and less forgiving of ordinary tap water. Marble, koi, and veil are color and fin forms of the common fish, and the longest fins are the first thing another cichlid shreds. Buy animals that are eating and holding the fins fully extended, not a pale fish clamped in the top corner of a wholesale vat. Ten to 12 years is a long time to keep a cichlid that will eventually refuse the tankmates you chose in year one.",
  ],

  "fish/apistogramma": [
    "Apistogramma is a genus of small South American cichlids, not one species with a single correct temperature. Most of the fish sold under the name stay 2 to 3 inches and live 3 to 5 years. The table's 72 to 86°F span is that wide because a cooler-water borellii and a warmer-water cacatuoides got printed on one card. Care level is intermediate. A 20-gallon tank is the listed minimum, and a long footprint matters more than height for a fish that lives on the sand.",
    "pH on the card runs from 5.0 to 7.0. Blackwater species, including many agassizii, fade and stop spawning in hard tap water. Some line-bred cockatoo cichlids, A. cacatuoides, tolerate closer to neutral. If the label says only Apistogramma, ask which species is in the bag before you set the heater. Fine sand, leaf litter, and a cave the female can fill with her body are the furniture. A bare tank with plastic plants gives her nowhere to put a brood, and the male spends his time displaying at the glass.",
    "Temperament is semi-aggressive, and almost all of that aggression is about sex and a cave. A female guarding eggs turns yellow and black and will drive the male off. The group that works in 20 gallons is one male and two or three females, not two males. Two males in a small tank fight until one stops eating. Any dither fish have to accept the same soft, acidic water. A barbs-and-goldfish community cannot be their room.",
    "They are omnivores that do better on small meaty foods than on large cichlid sticks. Baby brine shrimp, frozen daphnia, and a pellet they can chew match a 2-inch mouth. Males are the fish on the price tag: tall dorsal spines on a cockatoo, a spear tail and a cheek stripe on an Agassiz's. Females look plain until they spawn, and then they are the brightest animal in the tank. Buying only the male is why he never quite colors up.",
    "A dark cave and soft water are what produce a spawn. The female fans the eggs. Fry need microworms or newly hatched brine shrimp, and any danio in the tank will eat them. Three to 5 years is a short life next to a big Central American cichlid, so a hollow-bellied import has already used part of it on a breeder rack. These are not dwarf cichlids for a goldfish tank or a rift-lake tank. Both of those run outside the pH on this card.",
    "Shops mix the names. A Macmaster's, a cockatoo, and an Agassiz's do not want the same hardness, and a German blue ram is not in this genus at all. Set the heater for the species you actually bought, somewhere inside the 72 to 86°F band, instead of parking it at the top because the card allows it. Hot water and a tired filter is how a fish listed for 3 to 5 years dies in the first one. Nitrate under 20 ppm, ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm, and a cave she can defend are the practical test.",
  ],

  "fish/arowana": [
    "The arowana people actually keep is the silver, Osteoglossum bicirrhosum, from the Amazon. Asian arowana, Scleropages formosus, is a different fish and is listed on CITES Appendix I. The table covers Osteoglossum and Scleropages together, with adult size at 24 to 36 inches and some individuals larger, and a lifespan of 15 to 20 years or more. The tank floor on this page is 250 gallons. A juvenile in a 55-gallon tank is a temporary situation with a deadline, not a home.",
    "Temperature sits at 75 to 82°F, pH 6.0 to 7.5, hardness about 4 to 12 dGH. Ammonia at 0 ppm is marked critical for a reason. A fish this size produces a heavy bioload, and a dirty tank shows up as frayed fins and refusal to eat. They are carnivores and predators. Fish, insects, and meaty frozen foods are the diet. Feeder goldfish are a poor staple. They carry parasites and teach the arowana to ignore prepared food.",
    "They are jumpers. The eye is built to look up, and a gap at the lid, a loose cable, or a hood left open during feeding is enough. Arowana leave the tank and dry on the floor. The lid has to be heavy, tight, and closed every time. Temperament is predatory and semi-aggressive. Anything that fits in the mouth is food, including smaller tankmates that were the same length on the day they were added. Bottom fish that stay out of the mouth can work. A school of tetras will not.",
    "Silver arowana are the legal, commonly sold pet. Asian arowana paperwork, when a fish is even legal to possess, is part of buying that species, not an optional extra. Do not take a shop's word that a red or gold juvenile is a silver that will color up. The body shape and the chin barbels differ, and the legal status differs. A fish you cannot document is a fish you should not bring home.",
    "Growth is the bill people do not run before the purchase. A 4-inch silver becomes a 2-foot fish that needs the 250-gallon minimum and a filter to match, and it may live past 20 years. Expert is the care level on the card. That reflects the adult, the jump risk, and the predatory diet, not the week a juvenile spends eating pellets in a shop tank. Plan the tank you will need in three years, or do not buy the fish.",
  ],

  "fish/assassin-snail": [
    "Assassin snails are Clea helena, also sold as Anentome helena, a small Southeast Asian snail that hunts other snails. Adults are about 0.75 to 1.25 inches and live 2 to 3 years. This card starts them at 10 gallons. They are not a plant-safe algae crew that happens to eat a pest on the side. The mouth is a proboscis used on meat. A tank bought an assassin to clean glass will still have dirty glass.",
    "They want 72 to 78°F, pH 7.0 to 8.0, general hardness 8 to 15 dGH, and carbonate hardness 5 to 12 dKH. Soft, acidic water eats at the shell of any snail, and this one is no exception. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm and nitrate under 20 ppm keep them active. They bury in sand. A thick gravel bed makes them harder to find and gives pest snails more places to hide from them. A sand substrate shows both the hunter and the shells.",
    "The diet is other snails and carrion. They will take a bladder snail, a ramshorn, and a dead fish or a leftover bloodworm. They do not wipe out a huge snail explosion overnight. A tank with hundreds of small snails needs more than one or two assassins, and even then the cleanup takes weeks, not a weekend. Meanwhile the pests keep laying eggs. Remove egg masses by hand if the outbreak is already out of hand. The snails are a slow pressure, not a reset button.",
    "They can breed in a freshwater tank. The card calls breeding moderate, slow but steady. A keeper who adds six of them to solve a pest problem can end up with a population of assassins once the easy food is gone. They also take other ornamental snails. Nerites, mystery snails, and rabbit snails are not safe roommates. If those animals are the point of the tank, leave the assassin out.",
    "Care level is listed as easy, and the adult animal is undemanding if the water is hard enough and some meat reaches the bottom. The mistake is the job description. They will not replace a nerite on algae, they will not save a planted tank from a snail bloom in a day, and they will eat the snails you paid for. Use them when pest snails are the target and every other snail has already been moved.",
  ],

  "fish/bamboo-shrimp": [
    "Bamboo shrimp are Atyopsis moluccensis, the fan shrimp from Southeast Asia. Adults reach 2 to 3 inches and live 5 to 8 years, a long life for a freshwater shrimp. Give a fan shrimp at least 20 gallons. They are filter feeders. The fans on the front limbs open in a current and strain fine particles out of the water. A shrimp sitting in a still corner with its fans folded is a shrimp that is not eating.",
    "Keep them at 74 to 82°F, pH 6.5 to 7.5, general hardness 6 to 15 dGH, and carbonate hardness 3 to 10 dKH. The card also asks for high oxygen and a well-aerated tank, which fits an animal that stands in a flow. Keep ammonia and nitrite at zero, and hold nitrate under 20 ppm, or the fans stay closed even when the current looks right. A powerhead or the outflow of a filter, aimed so the shrimp can perch in it, is part of the furniture. A bare tank with a huge filter and crystal-clear water is how these shrimp starve.",
    "The food has to be suspended. Crushed flake, powdered fry food, or a bit of spirulina dusted into the current reaches the fans. A pellet that drops straight to the sand does not. They are not algae-eating Amano shrimp, and they will not clean a planted tank. In a tank that is over-filtered and never fed fine food, they slowly empty out. A starving bamboo shrimp looks pale, stays in a low-flow spot, and stops opening the fans. By the time that is obvious, the animal is hard to bring back.",
    "Breeding is marked very difficult because the larvae need brackish water, the same wall Amano shrimp hit. Adults live and feed in freshwater. A female with eggs is not about to stock the tank with babies you can raise beside her. Plan on the adults you bought, not on a colony. One shrimp can live alone. Two or three in a 20-gallon tank need more than one perch in the current or they will crowd the same outflow and the smaller one will stop feeding.",
    "Care level is intermediate because of the feeding, not because the water is exotic. Five to 8 years only happens if the fans stay busy. Add the shrimp to a tank that already has a little suspended debris and a place to stand in the flow. A brand-new, brilliantly clear setup with a canister filter and no target feeding is the setup that kills them while the fish look fine.",
  ],

  "fish/blenny": [
    "Blenny is a family, Blenniidae, not a single marine fish with one diet. The animals sold under the name run about 2 to 6 inches depending on species and live 4 to 6 years. Tank size on the card is 20 to 55 gallons or more, again by species. They come from tropical and temperate seas, and in a home reef they need 72 to 82°F, pH 8.1 to 8.4, and a specific gravity of 1.020 to 1.025. A reef with readable ammonia, or nitrate past 20 ppm, loses blennies while the corals still look acceptable.",
    "The diet line says herbivore to omnivore because the family really does split. A lawnmower blenny, Salarias fasciatus, grazes film algae and starves in a new tank that was scraped clean and then stocked with a hungry blenny on day one. A midas blenny will take prepared foods. A fang blenny bites fins and mucus and is not a cleanup animal at all. Read the species on the tag before you trust the word blenny. The care level easy applies to the common algae grazers in a mature tank, not to every fish in the family.",
    "Some blennies are peaceful grazers and some are semi-aggressive. Most of the algae-grazing species want a hole they can back into, and two of them will fight over one favorite burrow. A lawnmower that has eaten the visible algae may start picking at coral mucus or at clam mantles. That is hunger, not a sudden change of personality. Feed nori and algae-based foods once the rocks are clean, or the fish declines while the rest of the reef still looks fed.",
    "Blennies are not gobies. They lack the fused pelvic sucker gobies use to perch, and many species wear fleshy cirri over the eyes that look like eyebrows. They hop and perch on rock rather than hovering in open water. A lid still matters. Startled blennies leave the tank through surprisingly small gaps. A 20-gallon minimum fits a small species. A fish headed for 6 inches belongs in the upper end of the 20 to 55 gallon range, with more than one crevice.",
    "Buy a blenny with a full belly and algae already growing in the tank it is going into. A thin lawnmower in a bare quarantine tank needs algae sheets or it wastes. Four to 6 years is a fair life for a fish that spends its day scraping rock, and it only happens when the diet matches the species. An omnivore label on a family card is not permission to feed flake to a grazer and call it done.",
  ],

  "fish/blue-green-chromis": [
    "Blue green chromis are Chromis viridis, a damselfish from Indo-Pacific reefs and the Red Sea. Adults reach 3 to 4 inches and the lifespan line is 8 to 15 years, which is long for a fish people treat as a cheap school. They need 72 to 82°F, pH 8.1 to 8.4, specific gravity 1.020 to 1.025, and a tank of at least 30 gallons. Nitrate under 20 ppm, with ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm, keeps the color from muddying. They are peaceful schooling fish, and a single animal is the wrong social unit.",
    "On the reef they hang above coral heads and pick zooplankton from the current. In a tank that means a small marine pellet and frozen mysis or brine shrimp, not a diet of nori meant for a tang. They are omnivores, so an algae-based flake can be part of the rotation, but meaty frozen food is what puts weight on a fish that arrived thin from a wholesale system. Feed them where the school is, in the open water, or the food hits the sand and the wrasses get it.",
    "Color is the husbandry tell. A healthy school reads blue-green and shifts as the fish turn. A chromis that has gone olive-brown is stressed, underfed, kept alone, or sitting in rising nitrate. The change is easy to blame on the light. It is usually the group size or the water. Buy at least six, and buy them together. A pair will harass a third fish that was added a month later. The peaceful label assumes a real school with enough room to spread out over the rock.",
    "They are reef-safe in the ordinary sense. They do not eat coral or ornamental shrimp. They do jump when the school spooks, so the lid or the mesh screen has to be on. A 30-gallon tank holds a small group and gets tight once the fish are a full 4 inches and sharing the rock with larger fish. They occupy the upper half of the tank. A goby on the sand and a chromis school above it is a better split than two midwater species fighting for the same open water.",
    "Eight to 15 years is the part the price tag leaves out. A two-dollar fish that lives a decade still needs a stable reef for that decade. Replace losses in the school promptly. A chromis left alone after its group dies does not become a centerpiece. It fades, hides, and stops eating. If the shop tank has one brilliant fish and five pale ones, the pale ones are the husbandry lesson, not a bargain.",
  ],

  "fish/blue-velvet-shrimp": [
    "Blue velvet shrimp are a blue line of Neocaridina davidi, the same species as the red cherry shrimp, with a wild ancestor from Taiwan and the color fixed in captivity. A velvet adult is about 1 to 1.5 inches and is old at 1 to 2 years. A tank of 5 gallons or more can hold a colony. They are listed as beginner animals and as prolific breeders. The blue is a grade, not a separate species, and it does not stay blue if you mix it with red, yellow, or wild-type Neocaridina.",
    "They prefer cooler water than a typical tropical community. The card lists 68 to 74°F, pH in a broad 6.5 to 8.0 band with a tighter 6.8 to 7.5 called out beside it, general hardness 6 to 8 dGH, carbonate hardness 2 to 5 dKH, and TDS around 150 to 250 ppm. A heater set to 80°F for a betta is already above their preferred band. Zero ammonia, zero nitrite, and nitrate under 20 ppm are the readings that keep a blue colony from crashing after a top-off. A 5-gallon tank has very little dilution, so the small minimum is not a low-maintenance bowl.",
    "They eat biofilm, soft algae, and leftover food. A shrimp-specific pellet or a blanched vegetable keeps a colony fed once the tank is too clean to graze. They are not a substitute for a bamboo shrimp, and they will not clear hair algae by themselves. Copper medications kill Neocaridina. Any treatment sold for ich that lists copper is a colony wipeout. Move the shrimp, or do not use the copper.",
    "Breeding really is easy if the parameters stay put. Females carry eggs under the tail for a few weeks and release tiny copies of the adults. There is no brackish larval stage, which is the difference between this species and an Amano. A 5-gallon tank fills up. Plan a place for extras before the first berried female, or the colony will stall when nitrate climbs and food runs short. One to 2 years is the adult life, so the colony persists by breeding, not by the original shrimp living a decade.",
    "Color fades for three reasons: poor food, a cross with another color line, and stress from heat or a dirty tank. If you want blue offspring, keep only blue velvet shrimp in that tank. A single red cherry dropped in as a hitchhiker will throw wild-type and muddy young within a generation. Males stay smaller and less opaque. Females show the saddle of eggs behind the head. A colony of only males will look tidy and then simply age out.",
  ],

  "fish/bolivian-ram": [
    "Bolivian rams are Mikrogeophagus altispinosus, a small South American cichlid that shops still mix up with the German blue ram. They are not the same fish. Adults reach about 3 to 3.5 inches and live 4 to 7 years, a longer span than the 3 to 4 years listed for Mikrogeophagus ramirezi. A pair can live in 20 gallons. Temperament is very peaceful for a cichlid, and the difficulty line says beginner. That peace is real. It is also why people drop them into tanks that are too hot, too soft, and too busy, copying ramirezi care by mistake.",
    "They come from the slower rivers and pools of the Bolivian and Brazilian Amazon drainages, not from the hot, mineral-poor water a German blue ram is pushed into. They do well at ordinary community temperatures and in moderately hard water. A heater cranked into the mid-80s, which a ramirezi tank often needs, is not a favor to this species. Treating a Bolivian ram as a German blue ram is how this fish gets kept wrong. A Bolivian ram that is restless, clamped, and losing weight in a very warm, very soft tank is often in water that was set up for its cousin.",
    "A 20-gallon tank holds a pair. Give them sand they can sift and a flat stone or a broad leaf if you hope to see a spawn. They are biparental. Both fish tend the eggs, and they do not rearrange the whole aquascape the way a convict does. They will still chase any fish that hovers over a clutch. Very peaceful describes day-to-day life with tetras, corydoras, and other small neighbors. It does not describe a pair with eggs in a 10-gallon tank of shrimp.",
    "Feed them as small omnivorous cichlids. A pellet they can chew, frozen daphnia, and bloodworms put more condition on a ram than flakes alone. They pick at the sand. Food that is gone in the first second, stolen by faster danios, never reaches them. Target the lower water with a pipette or feed after the lights dim if the tank is busy. A Bolivian ram with a sunken belly in a tank of fat tetras is a feeding problem, not a mystery disease.",
    "Sexes are readable once the fish are grown. Males carry a longer, more pointed dorsal and stay a bit larger. Females are rounder and often show a warmer wash on the belly when they are ready to spawn. A shop pair that is actually two males will spar in a 20-gallon tank even though the species is peaceful with other kinds of fish. Give them a cave each, or be ready to separate them. They do not need the stacked rock of a mbuna tank, and they should not be kept in one. The pH and the company are both wrong.",
    "Four to 7 years is achievable because this species tolerates the small mistakes that kill German blue rams: a week of cooler water, a nitrate reading that drifted, a diet that was ordinary rather than perfect. That tolerance has a limit. They still want clean water, a sand bed, and no fin-nipping barbs. A Bolivian ram is a fair first cichlid if the tank is quiet and the fish is not being asked to live like a rift-lake mbuna or like a hot-water ramirezi. The body is deeper and the snout blunter than a German blue ram, and the electric-blue breeding lines sold as rams are the other species.",
  ],

  "fish/bristlenose-pleco": [
    "Bristlenose plecos are Ancistrus, a group of small South American suckermouth catfish. Adults stay about 4 to 6 inches and live 10 to 15 years. That size is the reason to buy one on purpose. A common pleco sold as a baby looks similar for a month and then heads past a foot long. The tank minimum here is 20 gallons. Temperature is 73 to 81°F, pH 6.5 to 7.5, hardness 4 to 15 dGH. Nitrate is listed under 30 ppm, a little more forgiving than some cards, and ammonia and nitrite still sit at 0 ppm.",
    "Males wear the bristles, a branched crown on the snout that gives the fish its name. Females may show a few short bristles at the edge of the snout or none at all. The difference matters if you keep more than one. Two adult males in a 20-gallon tank will fight over a cave. One male and one female is the pair that works in the minimum tank. A larger group needs more wood, more caves, and more than 20 gallons.",
    "The diet is herbivore and omnivore. They rasp biofilm and soft algae, and they need real driftwood in the tank. Ancistrus chew wood. A tank with no wood and a diet of fish flakes produces a thin pleco that spends the night on the glass looking for something that is not there. Blanched zucchini, a sinking algae wafer, and the wood itself are the staple. They are not a solution for every algae type. Black beard algae is mostly ignored. Green film and diatoms are what they actually reduce.",
    "Temperament is peaceful toward fish they cannot fit in the mouth. Fry and tiny shrimp are not safe once the pleco is grown. They are nocturnal. A fish you never see during the day is behaving normally if the glass is rasped clean by morning and the belly is round. A bristlenose parked on the heater, breathing hard, is not shy. Check the water before you decide it is hiding. They produce more waste than a fish of 5 inches looks like it should, and a 20-gallon tank with only a small hang-on-back filter will show it.",
    "Ten to 15 years means the cute juvenile is a long resident. Breeding is straightforward once a male owns a cave. He guards the eggs and the fry. If you did not plan on young plecos, give him a cave anyway, because a male with nowhere to sit becomes the fish that shoves the female around the tank. Care level is easy if the wood is present and the filter is honest. It is not easy if the animal was bought to replace maintenance in a tank that is already overstocked.",
  ],

  "fish/cardinal-tetra": [
    "Cardinal tetras are Paracheirodon axelrodi, the neon's larger, redder cousin from the upper Rio Negro. Adults are about 2 inches and live 4 to 5 years. The tank minimum is 10 gallons, which is enough for a small school and tight once you keep the group size these fish actually need. They want 73 to 81°F and a pH of 4.6 to 6.2. That pH is more acidic than a standard community tank. Hard, alkaline tap water is why a school looks brilliant in the shop and fades at home.",
    "The color split from a neon tetra is the whole body, not a red patch behind the dorsal. A cardinal carries the blue line and the red stripe from nose to tail. Neons stop the red about halfway. People buy cardinals, house them like neons in neutral water, and then blame the breeder when the red shrinks. Soft water, dim light, and a dark substrate are what keep the stripe. Leaf litter or a little botanicals can pull a hard tap-water tank down into the range. Chasing 4.6 with acid buffers in a tank you cannot test is how you crash it.",
    "They are peaceful omnivores and a schooling fish. Six is a bare group. Ten or more in a longer tank than the 10-gallon minimum is when they use the open water instead of hiding in one plant. A small pellet, crushed flake, and frozen baby brine or daphnia match a 2-inch mouth. They feed in the middle of the tank. Long-finned tankmates that nip, and cichlids large enough to eat a 2-inch tetra, are the wrong company. Angelfish and cardinals are a classic pair only when the angels are not yet large enough to swallow them.",
    "Wild-caught fish still make up much of the trade, and a new school is sensitive to a fresh tank. Add them after the filter is stable, not the week the aquarium is filled. Nitrate belongs under 20 ppm, and any ammonia or nitrite reading means the school waits. A cardinal that clamps its fins and loses the blue line in the first three days is usually ammonia or a pH the fish was not acclimated toward. Drip them over from the bag. Dumping a soft-water fish into a hard tank all at once is a reliable way to lose a few.",
    "Four to 5 years is the life if the school stays together and the pH stays soft. A lone cardinal in a community of boisterous barbs will not reach it. They are listed as beginner fish, and a captive-bred school in a planted tank with soft water behaves like one. A wild school in an uncycled 10-gallon tank with a high pH does not. The beginner label assumes the water is already in the range on the card.",
  ],

  "fish/celestial-pearl-danio": [
    "Celestial pearl danios are Danio margaritatus, the fish shops also call a galaxy rasbora. The rasbora nickname is a leftover from the year they were described. They are danios, family Cyprinidae, and they are not zebra danios. Adults are about 1 inch and live 3 to 5 years. A tank of 10 gallons or more, densely planted, is the minimum that lets a school behave like itself. Temperament is a peaceful nano fish. In a bare tank with fast roommates they simply vanish into a corner and stop showing the spots you bought them for.",
    "They come from small, vegetated pools in Myanmar, and the aquarium fish today are mostly captive-bred. The wild pools are cooler and quieter than a discus tank or a Lake Malawi setup. Keep them out of hot, hard, alkaline water. A planted community in the low to mid 70s, with gentle flow and a dark substrate, is the look that brings the pearly spots and the orange-red on the males out of hiding. A strong powerhead pins a 1-inch fish to the glass. Turn the flow down or baffle it.",
    "Males carry more red on the belly and a busier pattern of pearly spots. Females are rounder and plainer, with a dark little mark at the vent that is easier to see in a clear cup than in the tank. A school of eight or more, with both sexes, is when the males display instead of sulk. Six males and no females will spar in a way the peaceful label did not prepare you for. The fighting is small, but a beaten male stops eating, and at this size you notice only when he is already thin.",
    "The mouth is tiny. Crushed flake, a micro pellet, and frozen baby brine shrimp or cyclops are the foods that fit. A community wafer meant for a pleco is irrelevant to them. Feed in the planted midwater where they actually hunt. If danios or barbs share the tank, the celestial pearls get nothing. They should not share with boisterous barbs at all. A tiger barb is a different kind of animal, and a 1-inch shy danio is not a dither fish for it.",
    "They jump. A lid, or a full cover of floating plants plus a lid, belongs on the tank. The floating plants also dim the light, which is what gets a school out of the stems and into the open. Three to 5 years is a short life, and it is easy to waste the first months in a tank that is too bright, too fast, and too empty. They are listed as beginner fish. That is fair in a quiet planted 10-gallon tank and unfair in a community that was stocked for color and speed.",
    "Do not buy them as a substitute for zebra danios. Danio rerio is larger, faster, happier in cooler open water, and perfectly willing to nip a slow neighbor. Celestial pearls are the opposite animal that happens to share a genus. A shop that labels them galaxy rasboras is selling the same Danio margaritatus. A shop that offers them as food for an angelfish has misunderstood both fish. One inch is the adult size, not a juvenile measurement they will grow out of.",
  ],

  "fish/cherry-barb": [
    "Cherry barbs are Puntius titteya, a small barb from the shaded streams of Sri Lanka. Adults reach about 2 inches and live 4 to 6 years. They want 73 to 81°F, pH 6.0 to 7.5, and hardness around 4 to 15 dGH. Plan on 20 gallons before you buy a school. Temperament is peaceful, which already sets them apart from tiger barbs. They are omnivores and easy on the care card if the tank is planted and the group is large enough for the males to color up without bullying one fish.",
    "Male color is a husbandry result, not a strain you can buy and forget. A male kept over dark substrate, with plants and with females in the group, turns a deep red. The same male in a bare, brightly lit tank with three other males stays pale and picks fights. Females are tan with a dark lateral stripe and a rounder belly. Buy both. A tank of only males is why this peaceful barb has a reputation for nipping that the species does not earn in a mixed group.",
    "They live in the middle of the tank and use plants as breaks, not as a place to hide all day. A 20-gallon tank with a school of six to eight, a soft substrate, and some cover matches the way they behave. They are a poor choice for a tank of long-finned bettas or fancy guppies only if you keep too few males and nothing else for them to do. In a real school they mostly display at each other. Feed a small pellet, flake, and frozen daphnia or brine shrimp. They are unfussy omnivores and will also pick at soft algae.",
    "Sri Lankan wild stocks have been under pressure, and the fish in shops are typically captive-bred. That does not make a pale, clamped school a bargain. A cherry barb that arrives with a sunken belly in a wholesale tank of mixed barbs often carries the wear of that tank. Quarantine them where you can see them eat before they join a planted display. Hold nitrate under 20 ppm and do not introduce them into a tank that still shows ammonia or nitrite. They are hardy once settled. They are not immune to a new tank.",
    "Four to 6 years is realistic in a planted 20-gallon tank that stays in the middle of their temperature band. They do not need the heat of a ram or the alkaline water of a molly. A heater set to 82°F and a handful of crushed coral for livebearers is a different aquarium. Keep the cherry barbs with other peaceful fish that accept pH 6.0 to 7.5: small rasboras, peaceful tetras, and corydoras. They are the barb you can keep when you have already ruled the tiger barb out.",
  ],

  "fish/cherry-shrimp": [
    "Cherry shrimp are Neocaridina davidi, a Taiwanese shrimp bred into a red grade that now has a dozen trade names. Adults are 1 to 1.5 inches and live 1 to 2 years. Five gallons is the listed minimum for a starter colony. They breed easily and often. The red color is a line, not a different species from blue velvet, yellow, or wild-type Neocaridina. Mix the colors and the young come out brown and clear.",
    "The preferred temperature is cooler than most tropical fish: 68 to 74°F. pH is listed broadly at 6.5 to 8.0, with 6.8 to 7.5 as the tighter band, general hardness 6 to 8 dGH, carbonate hardness 2 to 5 dKH, and TDS around 150 to 250 ppm. A stable reading matters more than a perfect one. Swings kill more colonies than a pH that sits a little off the center of the range. For cherries, nitrate under 20 ppm with ammonia and nitrite stuck at zero is the line between a colony and a crash. A 5-gallon tank has very little dilution, so a dead snail or a missed fast can spike it.",
    "Copper in a fish medication will wipe a cherry colony out. That includes many ich treatments sold for community fish. If the tank has cherry shrimp, copper stays on the shelf. There is no safe guess at a low dose. Move the shrimp to another tank before any copper treatment, and do not move them back into a tank that still has copper in the silicone and the substrate. This is the practical rule that separates a shrimp tank from a fish tank that happens to contain shrimp.",
    "They eat biofilm, algae film, and leftover food. A colony in a mature planted tank needs little extra. A colony in a new tank needs a shrimp pellet or blanched spinach until the surfaces season. They are not bamboo shrimp and they will not fan-feed from the current. They also will not strip black beard algae. Females carry eggs under the tail and release fully formed miniature shrimp. There is no saltwater larval stage. A berried female in a tank with fish is mostly feeding those fish. Guppies, bettas, and any cichlid will take the babies, and many will take the adults.",
    "One to 2 years is the life of each shrimp. The colony lasts longer only because they breed. A tank of aging adults with no moss, no cover, and a hungry fish will look busy and then quietly empty. If you want red offspring, keep only the red line. A crystal red shrimp is the wrong addition. That animal is Caridina cantonensis and wants softer, more acidic water than this card. Cherries in crystal-red parameters often fail, and crystals in cherry parameters fail the other way. Pick one genus for the tank.",
  ],

  "fish/chili-rasbora": [
    "Chili rasboras are Boraras brigittae, a nano cyprinid that stays about 0.7 inches and lives 4 to 8 years if the tank suits a fish that small. The minimum is 5 gallons. Temperament is a peaceful nano fish, and the difficulty line runs from beginner to intermediate. The intermediate half is the food, the company, and the fact that a 0.7-inch fish disappears in a busy community. They are not a schooling tetra you can drop in with barbs.",
    "Boisterous barbs belong in another aquarium. A tiger barb, a rosy barb, even a fast zebra danio in a small tank, outcompetes them for food and keeps them pinned in the plants. The right tank is planted, dim, and quiet: other Boraras, a few ember tetras if the tank is larger than the minimum, and shrimp that will not be eaten because the rasbora's mouth cannot take an adult shrimp. Five gallons works for a species group. It does not work as a community.",
    "They come from the blackwater peat swamps of Borneo. Soft, acidic, tannin-stained water and a dark substrate are what turn a pale orange fish into the red one on the bag. Hard tap water keeps them alive for a while and keeps them washed out. Fine leaf litter and a gentle filter are more useful than a strong current. They hover in a loose group near the surface and the upper plants, not on the gravel. A school of eight or more is when that hovering looks like a species instead of a few lost specks.",
    "The mouth takes powder food, crushed flake, and tiny live or frozen foods such as baby brine shrimp and cyclops. A standard community flake is too big. If you feed only what your larger fish eat, the chilies starve in a tank that looks well fed. Four to 8 years is a long life for a fish under an inch, and it depends on that small food showing up often. They have a fast metabolism. A long weekend with an automatic feeder set for big pellets is not a feeding plan.",
    "Males are slimmer and redder, females rounder with less color. Spawning in a mature planted tank is possible and the eggs are scattered in fine plants. The adults will eat them. If you want to see fry, they need a separate heavily planted tank, not a promise from the display. Care is beginner-level only when the tank was built for them: stable soft water, no nippy neighbors, and food they can physically eat. A chili rasbora in a barb tank is an intermediate problem you created at the fish store.",
    "The common mix-up is with mosquito rasboras, Boraras brigittae versus B. merah and the other tiny Boraras. Brigittae has the dark wedge along the side and the intense red when it is kept correctly. If the shop cannot tell them apart, the care on this page still fits the group: tiny, shy, planted, and kept away from any fish that treats a 0.7-inch neighbor as a snack or a punchline. A 5-gallon tank still needs a lid and a sponge over the intake, because a fish this small rides out through a cable hole or disappears into the filter. Nitrate kept low, with no ammonia and no nitrite, is what lets one reach the 4-year end of the life span and maybe the 8. They are a species tank. They are not a dither fish.",
  ],

  "fish/chromis": [
    "Green chromis is the shop name for a small reef damselfish, almost always Chromis viridis, sold as a peaceful school. The card lists them at about 3 inches, with a lifespan of 8 to 15 years, in 72 to 82°F, pH 8.1 to 8.4, and a specific gravity of 1.020 to 1.025. A school needs at least 30 gallons. Nitrate on this card is under 10 ppm, tighter than a fish-only tank usually aims for. They are omnivores and they are peaceful only while the group is big enough to behave like a school instead of a pecking order.",
    "Buy a group, not a pair. Two or three green chromis in a 30-gallon tank sort themselves into a bully and a victim, and the victim fades, stops eating, and dies. Seven or more, added on the same day, spread that aggression out. A fish added later to replace a loss gets picked on. If you have to replace one, rearrange the rock when you add the newcomer so the old territories are gone. The beginner care level assumes you already know that. The label on the tank rarely says it.",
    "They feed in open water on zooplankton. A small marine pellet and frozen mysis or brine shrimp, fed in the water column, match that. Nori clipped to the glass feeds the tang and the snails. It does not feed this fish. A chromis with a pinched belly in a reef that looks well stocked is usually losing the race to faster fish, or it is the odd one out in a group that is too small. Watch the school eat. The fish that hangs under the overflow and never comes out is the one to separate, not the one to wait on.",
    "They jump when the school startles, and a 3-inch fish fits through a slot that looks too small. Mesh the top. They also ride the current and will end up stuck against an intake if the strainer is missing. Color tells you how the group is doing. Bright blue-green fish that swim together are settled. Olive, brown, or patchy fish are stressed, newly shipped, or being harassed. Give a new group a week of stable salinity before you judge the color. Specific gravity that swings between water changes will mute them faster than a dim light will.",
    "Eight to 15 years is a long time for a fish that costs less than a takeaway meal. The 30-gallon minimum holds a young school and gets small once the fish are full size and sharing the tank with larger angels or tangs. They use the upper water. Put bottom-dwelling gobies under them rather than another midwater school in the same 30 gallons. They are not blue damsels. A damsel bought as a starter fish often becomes the tyrant of the tank. A real school of green chromis should not, if the group was large enough from the first day.",
  ],

  "fish/cleaner-shrimp": [
    "The cleaner shrimp on this page is a saltwater invert, not a freshwater ghost shrimp and not an Amano. The animal most shops mean is the skunk cleaner, Lysmata amboinensis, white with a red stripe, about 2 to 3 inches long, living 2 to 3 years. The smallest reef on this card is 10 gallons. Keep the reef at 72 to 82°F and pH 8.1 to 8.4, specific gravity 1.020 to 1.025, nitrate under 10 ppm, and ammonia and nitrite at zero. Temperament is peaceful. Diet is omnivore, which means they do more than pick at fish.",
    "They set up a cleaning station and will pick dead skin and parasites off fish that choose to sit still. That behavior is real and it is not a treatment. A cleaner shrimp will not cure an ich outbreak, and it will not replace a quarantine tank. Fish that are hungry, or fish that eat shrimp, will eat the cleaner. Wrasses, hawkfish, and large predators are a bad roommate. A peaceful reef fish that ignores ornamental shrimp is the company that works.",
    "Feed them. Leftover mysis, a bit of mussel, and pellets that reach the bottom are the omnivore diet in practice. A shrimp that only gets what it can clean off healthy fish slowly starves in a tank with a light parasite load. They are scavengers with a party trick, not obligate cleaners. A shrimp that stays hidden and never comes out to a feeding stick is either molting or being hunted. Give it a rock crevice it can actually defend.",
    "Molting is normal. An empty shell on the sand, with a soft shrimp hiding nearby, means leave it alone. The new shell hardens in a day or two. Pulling the shrimp out to inspect it during a molt is how you kill it. Lysmata are simultaneous hermaphrodites, and a pair may carry green eggs under the tail in a mature reef. Raising those larvae takes separate equipment and a food culture. Eggs on a cleaner are not a promise of baby shrimp in the display. They are also not a sign that the shrimp is sick.",
    "Copper, and most medications aimed at marine ich, kill shrimp. A reef that may need copper later is a bad place to make a cleaner shrimp the centerpiece. Two to 3 years is the life on the card. They are listed as beginner inverts because a peaceful reef with stable salinity keeps them without much fuss. The beginner mistakes are copper, a hawkfish, and assuming the shrimp can live on parasites alone. A 10-gallon tank works for one or a pair and leaves little room for a fish that might eat them.",
  ],

  "fish/clown-loach": [
    "Clown loaches are Chromobotia macracanthus, a large social loach from the rivers of Borneo and Sumatra. The adult size on this page is 12 inches and up. They grow past a foot. Lifespan is 15 to 25 years. The tank minimum is 75 gallons, and that minimum is for fish that will keep growing for years after you stop thinking of them as juveniles. A 20-gallon tank is where people park them because the juveniles are 2 inches and striped and look like a small community fish. They are not that fish.",
    "They need a group. One clown loach hides, stops eating properly, and lives a smaller life than the species is built for. Three is a bare group. Five or more in a long tank, with caves and driftwood, is when you see them stack on top of each other and come out in the evening. Temperament toward other fish is peaceful and social. The aggression they do show is inside a group that is too small, or toward a loach that cannot get into the pile. They click. The sound is a click or a pop from the mouth, used when they squabble or when they are startled. A clicking loach is communicating, not choking.",
    "They are ich-prone. A temperature drop during a water change, a long trip home, or a tank that runs cool will bring white spots out on a clown loach faster than on the tetras next to them. Skinny disease, a wasting condition that leaves a sunken belly and a fish that eats and still thins, is a real problem in this species. A juvenile that arrives thin often never fills out. Do not buy the skinny one because it is cheaper. Quarantine the group and watch the waist behind the head for a week before they join a display. No home dosing chart fixes a fish that has been wasting for a month.",
    "The diet is meaty and ongoing. Sinking pellets, bloodworms, and the occasional snail are what keep a foot-long loach fed. They are often bought as a snail control. A group will reduce a snail population. It will also eat every ornamental snail you liked, and it will still need pellets after the snails are gone. They are not algae eaters. A clown loach rasping the glass is usually looking for a biofilm snack, not cleaning the tank. Sand is the substrate that spares the barbels. Sharp gravel wears them down on a fish that spends its life on the bottom.",
    "Fifteen to 25 years and a body past 12 inches is the planning problem. A 75-gallon tank is the listed floor, and a group of adults will use every inch of length. The filter has to move a lot of water without turning the tank into a raceway they cannot rest in. Caves must be bigger than the fish or they get stuck as they grow. Build the cave entrance so a thickening loach can still come out. Intermediate care is the right label. The fish is hardy when the group, the space, and the warmth are in place. It is a slow disaster in a small community tank.",
    "They hide. A new group may vanish into the wood for days and come out at night. That is normal. A group that never comes out, never clicks, and never touches food is not being shy. Check ammonia, look for ich on the fish you can see, and look at body condition. Pale, clamped fish with white spots need the temperature stable and the stress reduced before you reach for a medication bottle. Do not confuse the loach with a clown pleco, which is a small wood-eating catfish. If the shop animal is a striped bottom fish in a 2-inch body, ask which one it is before it goes into a 20-gallon planted tank. The loach will outgrow that tank and can live for two decades if it survives the outgrowing.",
  ],

  "fish/clown-pleco": [
    "Clown plecos are the small wood-eating loricariids, usually Panaqolus maccus or a close lookalike, sold with the same stripes as a baby clown loach and none of the same future. Adults stay about 3 to 4 inches and live 10 to 12 years. Twenty gallons is the smallest tank on this card. They want 73 to 82°F and pH 6.8 to 7.6. Temperament is peaceful. The diet line is the one to read twice: herbivore, and specifically wood.",
    "They need real driftwood. The fish rasp wood and digest the biofilm and the wood itself. A tank with resin ornaments and a stack of algae wafers is a tank where a clown pleco slowly starves while the glass stays dirty, because algae was never the main job. Blanched zucchini can be a side dish. It does not replace the wood. If you wanted an algae cleaner, an otocinclus or a bristlenose is the conversation. This fish is a wood chewer that happens to be striped.",
    "They are shy and mostly nocturnal. A cave or a stack of wood they can wedge under is where they spend the day. One clown pleco in a 20-gallon tank with other peaceful fish is a normal stocking. They do not need a school the way a clown loach does. Two males may argue over a single cave. Give each fish a retreat and the peaceful label holds. Shrimp and fry are usually ignored. A very small shrimp may not be, once the pleco is adult and the shrimp is a juvenile sitting on the same wood.",
    "Ten to 12 years is a long time for a fish you rarely see. Judge its condition at night or with a dim light: a round belly and a rasped patch on the wood mean it is eating. A clown pleco that gets thin while the bristlenose in the same tank looks fat is losing the wood, or the other fish is taking the wafers. Nitrate under 20 ppm, with ammonia and nitrite absent, is the water standard while the wood does the feeding. They are not hard to keep once the wood is in the tank. Beginner is a fair care level for that setup and a misleading one if the wood was left at the shop.",
    "The stripe pattern is bold when they are young and a little muddier with age. A pale, spotted pleco in the same tank is often a different species that was bagged as a clown. L-number plecos vary enormously in adult size. If the seller cannot say whether the fish eats wood and stays under 4 inches, do not put it in the 20-gallon tank this card describes. A common pleco juvenile is the expensive mistake hiding in that uncertainty. This page is the small one.",
  ],

  "fish/common-pleco": [
    "The common pleco in the hobby is the large suckermouth catfish sold under Hypostomus and Pterygoplichthys, not the bristlenose and not the clown pleco. This page lists adult size at 15 to 24 inches and lifespan at 10 to 15 years. The tank minimum is 125 gallons. Temperature runs a wide 72 to 86°F, pH 6.5 to 7.5. Temperament is peaceful. Diet is omnivore. Care level is printed as beginner. That last line is about a juvenile that survives neglect for a month. It is not about a two-foot fish.",
    "They are not an algae solution for a 20-gallon tank. A baby common pleco will eat some algae while it is small. Then it outgrows the tank, produces a heroic amount of waste, and needs food you actually provide: sinking wafers, vegetables, and a meaty offering, because the diet is omnivore rather than a pure algae clip. By the time the glass is too big for the fish to clean, the fish is too big for the stand. People rehome them into lakes, which is how Pterygoplichthys became a problem animal in warm waterways. Do not release one. Find a tank that fits or do not buy the fish.",
    "A 125-gallon tank is the floor on this card, and a 20-inch fish needs the length, not a tall column of water with no floor space. Driftwood and a cave it can actually enter as an adult belong in the setup from the start. Building a cave for a 4-inch juvenile and never enlarging it is how plecos get stuck. They are nocturnal. A fish you do not see until the lights go out is normal. A fish gasping at the surface is a water problem. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm and nitrate under 20 ppm take a real filter once the fish is eating like a 15-inch omnivore.",
    "Peaceful means they do not hunt other fish. It does not mean a small tankmate is safe from being sucked onto and rasped during the night. Slow, flat fish and fancy goldfish can lose slime coat to a large pleco that has decided to taste them. Fast midwater fish ignore them. Keep the pleco with fish that can stay off the glass at night, in a tank that was sized for the pleco rather than for the tetras.",
    "Ten to 15 years is the commitment hiding in a two-dollar juvenile. The beginner label falls apart at the first tank upgrade, which is not optional. If the shop cannot tell a common pleco from a bristlenose, look at the advice you were given about algae control in a small tank. That advice is the tell. A fish that stays 4 to 6 inches and wears bristles is the other animal. The one on this page is the fish that makes a 125-gallon tank the starting point.",
  ],

  "fish/congo-tetra": [
    "Congo tetras are Phenacogrammus interruptus, an African tetra in the family Alestiidae, not a South American tetra that picked up a river name. Adults reach about 3 to 3.5 inches and live 3 to 5 years. Thirty gallons is the smallest tank that gives the fins room. They are peaceful schooling fish, and the difficulty sits between beginner and intermediate because the males need room to carry their fins and the school needs length to swim. A 10-gallon community is the wrong shape even when the gallon count feels close on paper.",
    "Males grow trailing extensions on the dorsal, anal, and tail fins, and they flash bronze, blue, and red when the tank is dim and the water is soft. Females are plainer and fuller. The fins are the reason fin-nippers are out. Tiger barbs and serpae tetras will crop a male Congo down to a fish that no longer displays. Keep them with other peaceful midwater fish that will not harass those fins: larger rasboras, peaceful rainbowfish, and corydoras on the sand if the water suits both.",
    "They use open water. A tank that is wall-to-wall plants with no swimming lane makes a Congo tetra hide and fade. Plant the sides and the back, leave a front channel, and keep the light from blasting the tank white. They show color at dawn and dusk more than at noon. A school of six or more, with both sexes, is the minimum social unit. A pair in a 30-gallon tank spends its time behind the filter. Buy the group at once. Adding two pale females later to a settled group of males works better than adding a new male.",
    "The diet is an omnivore's diet with a bias toward small foods in the water column. A quality flake or small pellet, frozen daphnia, and brine shrimp keep the body deep and the color moving. They are not surface specialists and they are not bottom feeders. Food that sinks in one lump to the gravel misses them. Three to 5 years is shorter than people expect from a tetra this visible. A male with shredded fins in a busy community often does not reach the top of that range, because the stress is constant and low-grade rather than a single disaster.",
    "They come from the Congo basin and do best in soft, slightly acidic water with some tannin, not in a rift-lake tank. The African in the name confuses shoppers who have just bought mbuna. The water is the opposite. Hard, alkaline conditions mute the iridescence and shorten the easy years. Intermediate care is mostly this: a long tank, a real school, intact fins, and water that is gentler than cichlid salt and crushed coral. Beginner care is possible in a quiet planted 30-gallon tank that was actually set up for them.",
    "A Congo tetra that clamps its fins and loses the bronze sheen after a move is often reacting to a pH swing or to being the new male. Give the school time in stable water before you decide the color was a shop trick. Do not house them with angelfish once the angels can fit a tetra of this size in the mouth, and do not house them with any fish that nips. The fin extensions are the display and the weak point. If you want one showy male, you still buy the school. A single male in a mixed tetra tank is a shy, faded fish with nothing to display to.",
  ],

  "fish/convict-cichlid": [
    "Convict cichlids are Amatitlania nigrofasciata, a Central American cichlid from Guatemala, Honduras, and El Salvador. They are not African cichlids. Adults reach 4 to 6 inches and live 8 to 10 years. One fish needs at least 30 gallons. A pair needs 40 gallons or more. Temperature is 74 to 82°F, pH a wide 6.5 to 8.0, hardness 6 to 20 dGH. They are omnivores, care is listed as easy, and temperament is aggressive, especially when they breed. The easy part is keeping them alive. The hard part is everyone else in the tank.",
    "They breed readily. A pair will clean a cave, a clay pot, or a flat rock, lay eggs, and then treat the tank as a nursery with enemies in it. Fish that were tolerated yesterday get driven, bitten, and killed while the fry are out. The aggression is not a mood you can wait out. It lasts as long as the babies are being herded, and then the pair often spawns again. If you do not want fry, do not keep a male and a female together and hope they will refrain. They will not.",
    "A single convict in a large tank of robust fish can be a manageable pet. A pair in a community of tetras, angelfish, or shrimp is a scheduled loss. The 40-gallon pair tank should be their tank. Décor has to be solid. They dig, and they will uproot plants and shove gravel until a cave feels right. Stack rock so it cannot be undermined and topple. A cave they chose is a cave they will defend all the way to the glass.",
    "Feed them as omnivores that eat almost anything: a cichlid pellet, frozen foods, and vegetable matter so the diet is not only meat. They are greedy. Overfeeding a pair in a 40-gallon tank is how nitrate climbs past the 40 ppm mark this card allows as a ceiling. Ammonia and nitrite still need to be 0 ppm. Easy care assumes you can keep up with the waste of two fish that spawn and guard and eat. It does not mean a bowl or an unfiltered desktop tank.",
    "Eight to 10 years is long enough for many rounds of fry. Decide before the first spawn whether you have homes for the young or a species tank where predation and a plan replace surprise. The black bars on a gray body are the wild pattern. Pink and marble forms are the same species with the same temper when the eggs hatch. A convict that looks calm in a shop rack is a convict without a territory yet. Give it a cave and a mate and the card's aggressive line shows up on schedule.",
  ],

  "fish/corydoras": [
    "Corydoras are armored South American catfish, a genus rather than one fish. Depending on the species they reach 1 to 3 inches and live 5 to 10 years. The tank minimum is 20 gallons for a school. They want 72 to 78°F, pH 6.0 to 7.5, and hardness around 2 to 12 dGH. Temperament is peaceful and social. Diet is omnivore. The mistake that defines bad cory care is keeping one. A single cory is a nervous fish that never quite settles, even in a pretty tank.",
    "They are built to root in a group. Six or more of the same species, not a mix of leftovers that happen to share a family, is the school. Bronze corys do not fill the social need of a panda cory. Sterbai, aeneus, paleatus, and pygmaeus each school with their own. The barbels on the snout are sensory. Sharp gravel wears them down until the fish cannot find food and the mouth looks eroded. Sand, or a smooth fine substrate, is the bottom that matches the animal. They are not decoration for a cutting gravel bed.",
    "Corydoras breathe at the surface. A dash up, a gulp, and a dive back down is normal, not a sign that the filter has failed. It is also why a lid matters and why a film of dust or oil on the water is a problem. They still need oxygenated water. A hot, still tank at the top of their range, packed with fish, makes those dashes frantic. Their preferred band tops out at 78°F. A discus tank in the mid-80s is too warm for the common species on this card.",
    "Feed them on the sand. A sinking pellet, a tablet, and frozen bloodworms or daphnia after the lights dim reach the fish that wait until the tetras are done. Flakes that never leave the surface do not count as a cory diet. They are omnivores and they will pick through mulm. They are not algae eaters, and they will not clean a tank in the sense of replacing a water change. What looks like cleaning is foraging. If the only food in the mulm is rotten, the foraging makes the water worse.",
    "Five to 10 years is a real span if the barbels stay intact and the group stays together. A cory with red, worn barbels and a sunken belly is usually on the wrong substrate or losing every meal to faster fish. Beginner is a fair label for a sand-bottom 20-gallon tank with a school of one species and a filter intake they cannot get stuck in. It is a poor label for a single fish on crushed coral in a Rift Lake tank. The armor plates protect them from some bites. They do not protect them from bad company or from gravel.",
  ],

  "fish/crystal-red-shrimp": [
    "Crystal red shrimp are a graded color form of Caridina cantonensis, bred from a wild bee shrimp, not a red version of the cherry shrimp. Adults stay about 1 to 1.25 inches and live 1.5 to 2 years. Use at least 10 gallons so the soft water has a chance to stay still. Care is intermediate to advanced. Breeding is marked moderate and only works when the parameters stay still. They are more delicate than Neocaridina cherry shrimp. A keeper who has bred cherries has learned shrimp behavior and has not yet learned this water.",
    "The water is soft and acidic. Temperature sits at a cool 68 to 72°F. pH is listed at 5.8 to 6.8, with a tighter note of 5.8 to 6.6. General hardness is 4 to 6 dGH. Carbonate hardness is 0 to 1 dKH, which means the water has almost no buffer and can crash if you are careless. TDS around 100 to 150 ppm and nitrate under 10 ppm complete the card. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are assumed, and at this KH a small ammonia spike is harder to come back from. Active buffering soil is the usual way people hold those numbers. Inert gravel and tap water usually cannot.",
    "Stability is the actual care. A crystal red tank that swings two degrees and a few points of TDS at every water change will stall, fail to molt, or drop dead after looking fine. Use the same remineralized water each time, change a smaller slice more often, and match temperature before it goes in. They are a poor choice for a tank you are still learning to cycle. Ten gallons is the minimum because smaller volumes swing faster, not because the shrimp need swimming room.",
    "Feed lightly. Biofilm on seasoned wood and leaf litter should do most of the work. A dusting of shrimp food a few times a week is plenty for a small colony. Leftover powder that rots will lift nitrate past the 10 ppm line and stain the water in a way these shrimp tolerate badly. They are not a cleanup crew for a fish tank. Fish waste, fish medicines, and fish temperatures are the three reasons a mixed tank fails them. Copper kills Caridina as surely as it kills cherries. A treatment dosed last month can still be in the substrate.",
    "Grades, from basic red-and-white to high-coverage lines with inkier contrast, are appearance. A higher grade is not a hardier animal. If anything, the most selected lines are fussier. Females carry eggs under the tail for about a month, and the young hatch as miniature shrimp if the water stayed put. A failed molt, a white ring of death around the middle, usually means the GH or the TDS moved. One and a half to 2 years is the life. The colony continues only while breeding stays ahead of that clock.",
    "Do not add them to a cherry shrimp tank to see what happens. Cherries want higher hardness and a broader pH. Crystals in that water fade and die. Cherries in crystal water do poorly the other way, and a cross is not the project. Pick the genus, set the tank up for it, and season it before the shrimp arrive. Intermediate to advanced is the honest label. It describes the water, not the personality of an animal that spends its day picking at a leaf.",
  ],

  "fish/danio": [
    "The danio on this page is the zebra danio, Danio rerio, a 2-inch cyprinid from South Asia that lives 3 to 5 years. It is not a celestial pearl danio. A school fits in 10 gallons and not much else. Temperature is a cool 64 to 77°F, pH 6.0 to 8.0. They are peaceful omnivores on the card, and they are active enough that a small group in a short tank becomes nippy out of boredom. Beginner is a fair care level if you give them length, a school, and a lid.",
    "They are built for moving water and cooler tanks. A heater set for a betta at 80°F is above this card. They are one of the few common tropicals that stay active in an unheated room that sits in the high 60s. That does not make them a goldfish companion. Goldfish grow into a different bioload and a different social world. It does make them a bad roommate for a fish that needs the mid-80s. Do not put them in a discus tank and call it a dither.",
    "A school of six or more, in a tank longer than it is tall, is when zebras swim the length of the glass instead of orbiting one corner. The 10-gallon minimum holds that school and little else. They jump. Cover the tank, including the hole around the filter. Feed a flake or small pellet, plus frozen brine shrimp or daphnia. They eat at the surface and in midwater and they eat fast. Slower fish in the same tank need a second feeding lower down or the danios take it all.",
    "Peaceful has a footnote. A zebra danio with too few companions will nip fins, especially long fins on a betta, a fancy guppy, or an angelfish. The fix is the school and the space, not a lecture. They are also poor company for any fish small enough to be bullied off the food, including chili rasboras and celestial pearl danios. Same family, wrong roommate. A 2-inch zebra is a sprinter. A 1-inch galaxy danio is not.",
    "Three to 5 years is the life, and they often reach it because they tolerate a wide pH and a cool room. The failures are a bare bowl, a missing lid, and a pair kept alone until they shred each other's fins. Stripes run the length of the body. Long-fin and albino forms are the same species and a little less tough, because the fins are an easy target and the albino eyes like less glare. Wild-type short fins in a planted 10-gallon tank with a school are the beginner fish the card is describing.",
  ],

  "fish/discus": [
    "Discus are Symphysodon, the round, laterally flat cichlids of the Amazon basin. Adults reach 8 to 10 inches across that disc and live 10 to 15 years. The tank minimum is 55 gallons for a group. Care is advanced. They want 82 to 88°F, pH 6.0 to 7.0, hardness only 1 to 4 dGH, and TDS around 50 to 150 ppm. Nitrate is listed under 10 ppm and ideally near 0. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are marked critical. Temperament is peaceful but hierarchical. They are not a first community fish.",
    "The group is the social unit. One discus in a community of mixed tetras spends its life dark, tucked behind the heater, and slowly thin. Five or more, in a tall tank with room to turn a 10-inch body, is when a pecking order forms and everyone still eats. The hierarchy is real. The smallest fish will be kept off the food if you feed in one spot. Spread the offering, and watch the fish at the bottom of the order. A discus that is always the last to the pile and always hiding needs a different group, not another medication.",
    "Heat and soft water are the daily work. A tank at 84°F holds less oxygen and grows bacteria faster, so the filter and the water-change routine have to be sized for that, not for a cool livebearer tank. Large, frequent water changes are how keepers hold nitrate near the bottom of the range. Hard tap water has to be cut with reverse osmosis water and remineralized lightly. Guessing with peat and hoping is how the pH crashes overnight in a tank with almost no KH. Test before you celebrate a number.",
    "The diet is omnivore with the emphasis on protein. A quality discus pellet or granule, frozen bloodworms, and brine shrimp are the ordinary foods. Beefheart was an old staple and is not a requirement. A fish that eats only one frozen food and refuses pellets is a fragile fish to travel with. Offer prepared foods while they are young and still learning. Color forms, pigeon blood and blue diamond and the rest, are the same genus with the same water needs. A fancy strain is not a hardier strain. Many of the most selected lines are thinner and more easily spooked.",
    "They are poor roommates for cool-water fish, for nippy barbs, and for any cichlid that claims a cave and bites. A planted tank at 86°F is also a hard place to keep plants and a hard place to keep shrimp. Cardinal tetras are the classic dither because they can take the heat and the soft water. They still need a school and a tank bigger than a cramped 55 if the discus are fully grown. Ten to 15 years is the span if the group stays stable and the nitrate stays low. A discus that darkens, clamps, and stops eating is telling you the water or the hierarchy moved. Fix those before you shop for a cure.",
    "Advanced means the tank is already seasoned, the water source is understood, and you can change a lot of water on a schedule without swinging the temperature. It does not mean the fish is interesting to watch in a bare rack. Give them height, a calm room, and dimmer light in the afternoon. They are not a species you add to an existing community because the centerpiece died. Build the tank for the discus, then decide if any other fish can live in 82 to 88°F soft water beside them.",
  ],

  "fish/dwarf-crayfish": [
    "The dwarf crayfish on this page is the orange CPO, Cambarellus patzcuarensis, a Mexican crayfish that stays 1.5 to 2 inches and lives 2 to 3 years. A lidded 5-gallon tank is the smallest setup on the card. They want 68 to 75°F, pH from 6.5 to 8.0 with a practical band around 7.0 to 8.0, general hardness 6 to 12 dGH, and carbonate hardness 3 to 10 dKH. Care is easy and breeding in captivity is easy. The orange animal in the shop is a color form. It is still a crayfish.",
    "Small does not mean harmless. Dwarf crayfish nip. A slow fish that sleeps on the bottom, a betta with long fins, and a shrimp that molts in the open are all fair targets. They do better in a species tank, or with fast midwater fish that stay off the floor at night, than in a cherry shrimp colony. People buy them as a tiny lobster for a shrimp tank and then wonder where the shrimp went. The crayfish did not need to be large to do that.",
    "They climb. A lid, weighted or clipped, is part of the tank. An airline tube, a filter stem, and a plant that reaches the surface are all ladders. A crayfish on the floor dries out faster than a fish would, and it will try to leave. Give them a cave, a bit of wood, and somewhere to get out of sight when they molt. A molt looks like a dead crayfish. The live one is soft and hidden nearby. Leave both alone until the new shell hardens.",
    "Feed a sinking pellet, a blanched vegetable, and the occasional meaty offering. They are scavengers and they will also eat live plants if they are hungry or if the plant is tender. A heavily planted aquascape and a CPO is a negotiation. Java fern and Anubias usually survive. Delicate stem plants often do not. Two to 3 years is the life, and a colony replaces itself if a male and female are both present. Females carry eggs under the tail. The young are miniature crayfish, not a larval stage you have to move to salt water.",
    "The wild species is from the Lake Patzcuaro region and is in trouble there. The orange animals in the trade are captive-bred. That is the stock to buy, not a wild-caught crayfish of uncertain species. Do not release them. A 5-gallon tank with a lid, a cave, and no shrimp you are fond of is an honest home. Easy care is true for the water. It is not a promise that they will politely ignore every tankmate. If you wanted a gentle algae shrimp, this is the wrong basket at the shop.",
  ],

  "fish/dwarf-gourami": [
    "Dwarf gouramis are Trichogaster lalius, the small labyrinth fish formerly sold as Colisa lalia. Adults reach about 3.5 inches and the card gives them 4 to 6 years, 72 to 82°F, pH 6.0 to 7.5, and a 10-gallon minimum. They are omnivores and the temperament line says peaceful. The care line says beginner. That beginner label is the problem. Fish from commercial farms commonly carry dwarf gourami iridovirus. There is no treatment. A fish that fades, wastes, and dies months after you bought it often had the virus when it came home.",
    "Honey gouramis are a different species and a hardier one. They are not a color morph of the dwarf, and they do not stand in for it on this page. If the goal is a small peaceful gourami and the shop's dwarfs look clamped and pale, walk to the honeys. A dwarf that is already thick through the middle, eating, and holding color is the one worth a quarantine tank. Quarantine will not remove a virus. It will show you which fish are already failing before they enter a planted display.",
    "Males are the blue, red, and striped fish on the bag. Females are plainer and rounder. Two males in a 10-gallon tank will tear each other's fins even though the species is called peaceful. One male and one female, with plants that break the line of sight, is the pair that fits the minimum tank. They are labyrinth fish and they breathe air at the surface. Leave a gap in the floating plants and keep the air in the room from being much colder than the water, or a fish that comes up to breathe can damage its labyrinth organ on the chill.",
    "The diet is an easy omnivore diet: a small pellet, flake, and frozen daphnia or bloodworms. They feed slowly at the surface and in the upper plants. Fast danios and barbs will steal every meal. That is another reason the beginner community tank fails them, separate from the virus. A 10-gallon planted species tank, or a tank of quiet rasboras, matches the way they eat. They build bubble nests. A male spitting bubbles under a leaf is breeding behavior, not a filter problem. He will chase the female until she is ready, and in a small tank that chase can be too much. Give her cover.",
    "Four to 6 years is what the card promises and what the virus often cancels. There is no home remedy that fixes a dwarf gourami once it is wasting from iridovirus, and adding antibiotics aimed at bacteria does not touch it. Buy from a seller who can talk about the problem, prefer fish that have been in the shop long enough to show they are eating, and do not stack several males together because the colors look good in the bag. The species can be a calm, beautiful fish. The supply chain is why the care level on the card is optimistic.",
  ],

  "fish/electric-blue-acara": [
    "Electric blue acara are a blue captive line of Andinoacara pulcher, or a closely related electric-blue strain. They are not a wild electric species from a lake, and they are not a naturally occurring fish someone discovered already neon. Adults reach 6 to 7 inches and live 8 to 10 years. One fish or a young pair starts at 30 gallons. Temperament is peaceful for a cichlid, and the difficulty line says beginner to intermediate. The peaceful part holds until they pair and spawn. Then they harass any small tankmate that comes near the eggs.",
    "The blue was selected in aquaculture. Some lines are sturdy and some are thin, shy, and prone to sitting on the bottom with clamped fins. Buy a fish that is already eating and that has a full body, not the palest juvenile in a bare wholesale tank. A 30-gallon tank is the floor for one fish or a young pair and gets small once two 7-inch cichlids are guarding a brood. A longer tank, with sand they can sift and a flat stone or a piece of wood to spawn on, matches the way a pair actually lives.",
    "They are South American cichlids and they belong in neutral to slightly acidic community water, not in a hard alkaline Malawi tank and not with mbuna. The electric color does not make them an African cichlid. They also are not Jack Dempseys, green terrors, or oscars, even though a juvenile can look like a generic blue cichlid in a bad photo. Those fish get larger and meaner. An electric blue acara that is starting to terrorize a tank is usually spawning, not turning into a terror. The behavior drops after the fry are gone, and it comes right back at the next spawn.",
    "A pair will claim a patch of substrate, clean it, and drive tetras, shrimp, and smaller cichlids out of that half of the tank. In a 30-gallon community the driven fish have nowhere to go. Plan for that before you add a male and a female. One acara in a tank of robust mid-sized fish, such as larger tetras, a school of Congo tetras, or peaceful catfish, is the calmer stocking. Two of them are a future pair even if they ignore each other as juveniles. They dig. Plants rooted in the spawn site will be uprooted. Anchor the plants you mean to keep, or put them in pots.",
    "Feed a cichlid pellet and frozen or fresh meaty foods. They are eager eaters when they are healthy, and a fish that refuses food for more than a day or two while looking dark is a fish to isolate and watch. Eight to 10 years is a long life for a color form that some hatcheries push out weak. The beginner half of the care label fits a single healthy fish in a quiet 30-gallon tank. The intermediate half is the pair, the digging, and the week they spend trying to kill every neon tetra you thought was fine.",
    "Do not buy them as a rare wild import. If a seller describes an electric species from a named lake, the story is wrong. The color is a line. Treat the animal as a medium cichlid with a temper that changes when the eggs appear, give it sand and a solid place to lay those eggs, and keep the small, fragile tankmates in a different aquarium. A blue acara that spends its life with its fins clamped in a mbuna tank is in the wrong water and the wrong company, no matter how good the color looked under the shop light.",
  ],

  "fish/electric-blue-crayfish": [
    "Electric blue crayfish are a blue form of Procambarus alleni, the Florida crayfish, with the color selected in captivity. Adults reach 4 to 5 inches and live 5 to 6 years. One adult needs 20 gallons at the floor of this card. They want cooler water than most tropical fish, 65 to 75°F, pH 6.5 to 8.0, general hardness 6 to 18 dGH, and carbonate hardness 3 to 15 dKH. Nitrate is tolerated up toward 40 ppm on this card, which is not an invitation to skip water changes. Care is listed as easy. Breeding is easy in the right setup. The animal is still a crayfish that eats fish and plants and climbs out.",
    "Assume it will catch anything it can hold. Sleeping fish, fry, shrimp, and snails are food. A community tank with an electric blue crayfish in it is a community tank with a predator on the floor. Fast fish that stay at the surface sometimes last. The honest setup is a species tank. One crayfish in 20 gallons, with a cave and a sand bed, will use the space. Two adults may fight, especially two males, and the loser gets dismembered. The peaceful invertebrate people imagine is a different animal.",
    "They climb, and they are strong. A lid has to be clipped down. Filter stems, cables, and a corner the hood does not quite cover are exits. They also rearrange the tank. Plants are food and obstacles. A planted aquascape will be clipped and uprooted. Java fern tied to a rock may persist. A carpet of delicate plants will not. Driftwood and PVC or a clay cave give them the cover a crayfish uses when it is soft. They molt. The empty shell is not a body. The crayfish hides, often for a day or more, and should not be dug out to check on it.",
    "Feed them so they are not hunting out of desperation, knowing they will hunt anyway. A sinking pellet, a blanched vegetable, and a piece of fish or shrimp a couple of times a week keeps a 5-inch crayfish from living only on whatever wanders past. Overfeeding a 20-gallon tank is how the nitrate ceiling arrives early. They are messy. A filter rated for a larger aquarium is the one that keeps up. Cool water helps. A heater set to 80°F for a tropical community is outside this card and shortens the easy years.",
    "Five to 6 years and a simple breeding life means babies are likely if a male and a female share the tank. The female carries eggs under the tail. The young will be eaten by the adults if they have no cover, and they will eat each other. A species tank with leaf litter and many hides can raise a few. A tank you were not trying to breed will still surprise you. Do not release the extras. Procambarus alleni is a Florida animal, and a blue pet crayfish does not belong in a local pond. Easy care describes a lidded 20-gallon tank kept cool and fed. It does not describe a reef, a shrimp colony, or an open-top aquascape.",
  ],

  "fish/electric-yellow-cichlid": [
    "Electric yellow cichlids are Labidochromis caeruleus, the yellow lab, a mbuna from Lake Malawi. Adults reach 4 to 5 inches and live 6 to 10 years. The card's floor is 30 gallons, already tight for mbuna. Temperament is peaceful for a mbuna, and the difficulty line says beginner. Peaceful for a mbuna still means a rocky Malawi tank, hard alkaline water, and no South American community fish. It does not mean a yellow lab can live with angelfish, cardinal tetras, discus, or rams.",
    "The water is the same family of water as the rest of the rift-lake fish. Hard and alkaline, warm enough for Malawi, with rockwork that gives every fish a cave. A 30-gallon tank is the listed floor and it is small once you add the group a mbuna needs. One male and several females is the pattern that keeps the male from harassing a single female to death. Two males in 30 gallons will fight. The beginner label fits a keeper who already runs a Malawi tank and adds labs as one of the calmer species. It does not fit a keeper who is about to put one lab in a community of mollies and neon tetras.",
    "Wild Labidochromis caeruleus are not all yellow. The popular aquarium fish is the yellow population, the one associated with Lion's Cove, and other localities are white or blue. The fish on this page is the yellow one. Males develop more black in the fins and a slightly heavier head. Females stay yellow and a little smaller. Both sexes are bright, which is why shops sell groups of unsexed juveniles and why those groups later turn out to be mostly males. Ask, or buy enough that you can rehome the extra males when they color up and start digging pits at each other.",
    "Feed them as mbuna, which means a herbivore-leaning diet even though they are less specialized than some rock grazers. A spirulina-based cichlid flake or pellet, with only occasional meaty foods, keeps the gut in better shape. A diet of bloodworms and beefheart is how yellow labs bloat. They graze all day. A single heavy feeding and a long fast does not match the way they pick at aufwuchs on rock. Offer smaller feeds. The rockwork should have some growth on it. A bleach-clean pile of new stone in a new tank is a hungry tank.",
    "They are less aggressive than many mbuna, and they still rearrange a pecking order every time you add a fish. Introduce the group together. A lab added to a settled mbuna tank needs new rockwork on the same day or it will be pinned in a corner. They do not belong with South American cichlids. The pH those fish want will make a lab miserable, and the lab's water will make the South Americans miserable. Six to 10 years is a fair life in a hard-water tank with the right group. A single yellow lab in a soft-water community often lives a shorter, duller version of it.",
    "Thirty gallons is a minimum, not a suggestion to fill the tank with every mbuna that is on sale. A lab, a few females, and rock from end to end is already a full 30. If you want a mixed mbuna community, the tank has to grow. Do not add a pleco as an algae solution and forget that a common pleco will outgrow the same 30 gallons by an order of magnitude. A bristlenose is the pleco that can share a Malawi tank if you insist on one. The yellow lab itself will not keep the glass clean. Beginner, on this card, means the species is tougher and calmer than a Melanochromis. It still means Malawi water and Malawi rules.",
  ],

  "fish/ember-tetra": [
    "Ember tetras are Hyphessobrycon amandae, a nano characin from Brazil that stays about 0.8 inches and lives 2 to 4 years. Ten gallons is the floor for a school this small. Temperament is a peaceful nano fish and the care level says beginner. Two to 4 years is a short life next to a cardinal tetra. The beginner label fits a planted tank with a school of their own kind. It does not fit a barb tank, and it does not fit a tank where they are expected to be the dither fish for a cichlid.",
    "Barbs that shove and nip have no place beside an ember tetra. A tiger barb or a rosy barb will outcompete and stress a fish that is under an inch. Ember tetras belong with other small peaceful fish: chili rasboras in a tank large enough for both schools, small corydoras such as pygmaeus, and shrimp if you accept that fry may be eaten. A 10-gallon tank is a species tank or a very quiet nano community. It is not a community in the sense of one of each colorful fish.",
    "They show color in dim light over a dark substrate, in a tank with plants and some tannin. A bare, bright tank turns them a pale washed orange and keeps them glued to one clump of moss. A school of eight or more is when they spread out and flicker. Fewer than that and they hide. Males are slimmer and a deeper orange. Females are rounder. Spawning in fine plants happens in a mature tank, and the adults eat the eggs. Separate the breeders if you want fry. The display tank will not raise them for you.",
    "The mouth is small. Micro pellets, crushed flake, and baby brine shrimp or cyclops are the foods that fit. Feed a little, often. A 0.8-inch fish with a two-day gap between large feedings loses condition fast, and the lifespan is already only 2 to 4 years. They occupy the middle of the tank. Surface feeders and bottom feeders can share the same water if nobody is a nipper. Watch the embers at feeding time. If they never reach the food, the tank is too busy for them.",
    "They are often confused with juvenile flame tetras or with small serpae tetras. Flame tetras get larger and more aggressive. Serpae tetras nip fins. An ember stays tiny and, in the right tank, ignores its neighbors. If the shop fish is already chasing tankmates in the sales tank, it is probably not this species. Buy the school from a tank where they are shoaling together and already orange, not from a mixed tetra vat where one pale fish might be something else.",
    "Two years is the low end of the life span and four is a good outcome, which is why the water and the company have to be right in the first week. A missing lid, an open filter intake, or ammonia in a new 10-gallon tank is how a school shrinks. Even a small tetra will ride a ripple out of an open rim, so cover the tank and give them floating plants to sit under. Keep the current gentle. A powerhead meant for a hillstream loach will exhaust an ember. Soft light, small food, and a real school are what get them to the better end of 2 to 4 years.",
  ],

  "fish/endler-livebearer": [
    "Endler's livebearers are Poecilia wingei, a small poeciliid from Laguna de Patos in Venezuela. Males reach about 1 inch, females about 1.5 inches, and they live 2 to 3 years. The card allows 5 gallons and recommends 10 or more. They want 72 to 82°F, pH 7.0 to 8.5, and hard water, 10 to 25 dGH. They are peaceful, active omnivores, and they are easy only if you have already decided what to do with the fry and whether any guppies live in the same house.",
    "They hybridize with guppies. A male guppy in an Endler tank, or a female Endler in a guppy tank, will produce crosses, and the line stops being Poecilia wingei. If you care about keeping the species, the two fish do not share a tank, a net, or a fry grow-out tub. If you do not care, the hybrids are still livebearers and they will still fill the tank. The color on a male Endler is smaller and tighter than a fancy guppy's tail. A huge delta tail on a fish sold as an Endler is a hint that guppy is already in the background.",
    "If you keep both sexes, give the males cover and keep more females than males. A single female with several males is chased until she is exhausted. Plants at the surface, moss, and a broken line of sight are the cover. One male with two or three females in a 10-gallon tank is a calmer ratio than the reverse. An all-male group avoids fry and still displays. They will spar. In a planted tank the sparring stays visual. In a bare 5-gallon tank it becomes nipping.",
    "They are livebearers, and a female can drop a brood every month. Five gallons fills up. The fry are large enough to survive in the plants if the adults are well fed, which means the population problem arrives even when you were not trying to breed them. Have a plan before the first brood, not after the twentieth fry is hiding in the moss. Feed a small flake, a vegetable-based flake, and frozen brine shrimp. Hard water and a varied omnivore diet keep the males colored. Soft, acidic blackwater is the wrong tank. That water is for the tetras, not for this fish.",
    "Two to 3 years is the life, and it is easy to spend it looking at a tank of hybrids you did not mean to make. Buy from someone who keeps them away from guppies if the species matters to you. Nitrate under 30 ppm is the ceiling on this card. A crowded livebearer tank hits it. Easy care assumes you will change water as the biomass climbs, and that you will not add them to a betta bowl and call a 5-gallon minimum a bowl. They are active. They need the length of a real small tank, a lid, and more females than males whenever both sexes are present.",
  ],

  "fish/fancy-goldfish": [
    "Fancy goldfish are the round-bodied, coldwater fish: oranda, ryukin, ranchu, and the other double-tail forms. They are not single-tail comets or commons. Adults reach about 6 to 8 inches and live 10 to 15 years. The tank minimum is 20 gallons per fish. Temperature sits at 65 to 75°F, pH 6.0 to 8.0. They are peaceful omnivores. The beginner label is only true if the tank is filtered, cool, and bigger than a bowl. An unfiltered bowl is not a home for this fish.",
    "The round body is the husbandry problem. The same stout shape that makes a ranchu or an oranda look the way it does crowds the swim bladder and the gut. They are prone to buoyancy problems. A fish floating sideways or stuck at the surface may be reacting to dry floating food gulped with air, to a temperature swing, or to a deformity that was bred in. Soak pellets before you feed them. Skip the cheap dry flake that they snap at the surface. Sinking foods, gel foods, and a blanched vegetable reduce the air they swallow. Buoyancy trouble that persists is a reason to talk to a vet who sees fish, not a reason to add a tonic.",
    "They need more filtration than a single-tail comet of the same length. The body is compact, the waste is not, and a 20-gallon tank for one fish still needs a filter rated well beyond 20 gallons plus regular water changes. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm and nitrate under 20 ppm are the limits, and a fancy goldfish can blow past them after a heavy feeding. Twenty gallons per fish is the floor. Two fancies in a 20-gallon tank are already over the card. A comet can live a different, faster life in a pond. A ryukin cannot keep up with it and should not be asked to.",
    "Keep them cold. A tropical community at 78°F is above this card and shortens the life of a fish that can live 10 to 15 years in cool, clean water. Room temperature in a house that stays under 75°F often works without a heater. A heater set for a gourami does not. They are peaceful with each other and with other cool, slow fish of similar size. They will eat small tankmates and they will mouth plants. A planted aquascape and a fancy goldfish is a short story. Smooth ornaments and sand they cannot choke on are safer than sharp gravel.",
    "The wen on an oranda grows over the head and can cover the eyes. A fish that startles when you approach from the side, or that misses food, may not be able to see. Keep the wen clean of debris and do not pick at it. Ranchu lack a dorsal fin and are even less athletic. Do not house the most extreme body shapes with faster fancies that take all the food. Ten to 15 years only happens in a filtered tank that stays cool and gives each fish its 20 gallons. The bowl kills them through ammonia and heat, not through a lack of affection. Beginner care starts with the filter.",
  ],

  "fish/fiddler-crab": [
    "Fiddler crabs are Uca, a group of coastal crabs, not a fully aquatic pet and not a reef animal. Adults are about 2 to 3 inches including the legs and live 2 to 3 years. The setup is a paludarium of at least 10 gallons, with land and with water. Temperature is 75 to 85°F. The water is brackish, not fresh, at a specific gravity of 1.005 to 1.015 and a pH of 8.0 to 8.3. Humidity above the water needs to stay at 75 percent or higher. Care is moderate. Breeding is very difficult in captivity. A freshwater fish tank will drown them or slowly poison them with the wrong salt level.",
    "They need land they can walk on and air they can breathe. A deep pool with a rock sticking out is not enough if the crab cannot get fully clear of the water and stay there. Build a shore, or a sloped sand bank, and keep the water shallow enough that a crab on its back can find the bottom and flip. They drown in a full aquarium. The gill chamber has to stay wet, which is why the air humidity matters, and it also has to be able to breathe air. Both halves of the tank are the enclosure.",
    "Males carry one enlarged claw. That claw is for display and for argument with other males, not for catching fish. They are scavengers and grazers. A pellet, a bit of vegetable, and a little meat will be dragged onto the land to be eaten. They will also pick at each other when the tank is crowded or the food lands in one pile. A 10-gallon paludarium holds a small group if there is more than one burrow. They dig. Sand deep enough to burrow is part of the setup. Gravel over a bare glass bottom is not.",
    "Brackish means a measured salt mix, not a handful of table salt and not a marine reef at full salinity. Specific gravity 1.005 to 1.015 is the band on the card. Fresh water, which is what they get when someone adds them to a community tank, is outside it. Full-strength reef water is outside it the other way. They are not reef crabs, and they will not live among corals. Top off evaporation with fresh water so the salt does not creep up, and replace salt when you change water out. Guessing by eye is how the salinity doubles.",
    "Two to 3 years is the life if the shore stays damp, the water stays brackish, and they can molt in a burrow without being dug up. A crab that is about to molt often disappears. Leave it. Pulling it out to check is how a soft crab dies. Moderate care is the land, the salt, and the humidity. None of those are present in a cup of freshwater at a fair, which is where a lot of these crabs start. If that is the animal you were handed, it still needs the paludarium on this card, not a fish bowl.",
  ],

  "fish/flowerhorn": [
    "Flowerhorns are man-made hybrids, produced in Malaysia and Taiwan, with no scientific species name. They are not a wild fish from a lake. Adults reach 10 to 16 inches and live 10 to 12 years. An adult belongs in 75 gallons or more. They want 80 to 86°F, pH 7.0 to 8.0, and hardness around 8 to 15 dGH. They eat meat and prepared cichlid foods. Temperament is highly aggressive. Care is intermediate, mostly because the aggression and the waste are predictable. The fish is not a community animal.",
    "The nuchal hump, the kok on the forehead, is a breeding trait that was selected for, not a sign of illness and not a wild-type adaptation to a river. Different lines grow different humps. A hump that suddenly shrinks can mean a stressed or underfed fish. A hump that was never large may simply be the line you bought. Chasing a bigger hump with heavy feedings of one fatty food makes a messy tank and a fish with buoyancy trouble. Feed a cichlid pellet and frozen meaty foods on a schedule the filter can handle.",
    "They produce a heavy bioload. A 75-gallon tank with one flowerhorn still needs filtration sized for a dirtier fish than a single 12-inch body suggests, and nitrate under 20 ppm takes real water changes. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are the usual line and they slip fast after a big meal. One adult in the tank is the stocking. A pair will fight unless you are set up to breed and to separate them, and even then many keepers never house two adults together. Tetras, plecos, and other cichlids are targets. The peaceful tankmate stories usually end when the flowerhorn is no longer a juvenile.",
    "They recognize the person who feeds them and they will shove gravel around the tank for sport. Décor has to be solid and too big to swallow. They will drag heaters and uproot plants. A bare or sparsely decorated tank with a cave, if you offer one, is easier to keep clean than an aquascape. Intermediate care is the heat, the filter, and the decision to keep the fish alone. It is not a difficult water chemistry problem. pH 7.0 to 8.0 is ordinary hard tap water in a lot of houses. The difficulty is the adult animal.",
    "Ten to 12 years and a body that can pass 16 inches is the purchase. A 2-inch juvenile in a shop tank is a 75-gallon cichlid with a temper. Do not buy it for a community you like. Do not buy two because the colors look good side by side. The hybrid origin also means the exact adult size and the exact temper vary by line, within the range on this card. Budget for the large end of 10 to 16 inches and for a tank that can take the waste of a carnivorous cichlid held at 80 to 86°F.",
  ],

  "fish/french-angelfish": [
    "French angelfish are Pomacanthus paru, a marine angelfish, not the freshwater Pterophyllum that shares the word angel. Adults reach about 15 inches and live 10 to 15 years. The tank minimum is 250 gallons. Temperature is 72 to 82°F, pH 8.1 to 8.4, specific gravity 1.020 to 1.025, nitrate under 10 ppm. Care is expert. Temperament is semi-aggressive. Diet is omnivore. A juvenile in a 50-gallon reef is a temporary photograph, not a plan that satisfies this card.",
    "Juveniles are black with curved yellow bands. Adults darken to charcoal with gold-edged scales and a yellow face. The change takes years, and the fish is eating and growing the whole time. People buy the banded juvenile because it looks like a miniature of a poster and then discover the adult needs 250 gallons and will sample corals, sponges, and ornamental invertebrates. Reef-safe as a juvenile is not a promise about the adult. Plan on a fish-only tank with solid rock, or a reef you are willing to see nibbled.",
    "The omnivore diet in a tank means sponge-based angel foods, frozen mysis, and algae or nori, offered often. A French angel that eats only flake will be thin and will look harder at the corals and the clam mantles. Feed the angel specifically. In a mixed reef the wrasses and tangs get to the food first. Ten to 15 years is the life if the diet stays varied and the tank is actually large enough for a 15-inch fish to turn around. Expert care is the size, the diet, and the aggression toward other angels.",
    "One French angelfish is the usual stocking. Two adults fight unless they paired as juveniles and grew up together, and even then a 250-gallon tank is the starting size rather than a guarantee. They will harass other large angels, and a smaller angel added later is a target. Semi-aggressive on the card is mild language for a fish that owns a reef stack. Give it structure and open swimming room in front of the rock. A tall tank with no length makes a 15-inch angel pace the glass.",
    "Do not buy a juvenile you cannot house as an adult. Rehoming a 12-inch marine angel is a hard search, and the fish does not pause its growth while you shop for a 250-gallon system. The salinity and the pH are standard reef numbers. The expert label is not about mixing salt. It is about keeping a large omnivore that picks at invertebrates, lives for more than a decade, and needs a tank most people do not own when they see the yellow bands in a shop.",
  ],

  "fish/frontosa": [
    "Frontosa are Cyphotilapia frontosa, a deep-water cichlid from Lake Tanganyika. Adults reach 12 to 14 inches and live 15 to 25 years. This page starts them at 75 gallons. Temperament is listed as a peaceful giant. They are peaceful the way a large predator is peaceful toward fish it does not bother to hunt. They eat small fish. Difficulty is intermediate to advanced, mostly because they grow slowly, live a long time, and need Tanganyikan water rather than a community mix.",
    "They come from deep, dim water. The tank should be a long one with open sand, large rock caves, and light that is not a noon glare. They are not mbuna. A pile of small rubble and a crowd of aggressive rock-dwellers is the wrong layout and the wrong company. Hard, alkaline water, the kind Tanganyika actually is, is the chemistry. Soft South American water will not grow a frontosa out to a healthy 12 inches. Do not keep them with tetras, rasboras, shrimp, or any fish that fits in a 12-inch mouth. The peaceful label has a size limit, and the limit is the gape.",
    "Growth is slow. A juvenile that looks manageable for years is still on the way to 12 to 14 inches and may live past 20. The 75-gallon minimum is a young-adult tank and a tight one for a group. Frontosa are calmer in a group than alone, and a group of adults needs more length than 75 gallons provides. Plan the larger tank while the fish are small. They will not stay small because the current tank is small. They will stall, and a stalled frontosa is not the same thing as a healthy small adult.",
    "Feed a meaty sinking pellet and frozen or fresh seafood. They are not herbivores and they are not algae control. A diet of feeder fish is a parasite risk and a poor staple. They feed in a deliberate way and they feed low and midwater. Fast, aggressive tankmates will take the meal. The right neighbors, if any, are other Tanganyikan fish too large to swallow, in a tank that can hold everyone at adult size. A synodontis catfish is a common choice. A school of neon tetras is a snack.",
    "The bar count varies by locality. Burundi fish are associated with six bars, other populations with seven. The count is not a health indicator. A hump on the head develops with age, more in males. It is not a flowerhorn kok and it does not need special food to force it. Fifteen to 25 years means this is a fish you can still have when the rest of the original stocking is a memory. Intermediate to advanced care is the water, the patience, and the refusal to add small dither fish. They do not need dither. They need depth, dim light, and no one they can eat.",
    "A frontosa that hangs at the surface or pines in a bright, shallow, busy tank is in the wrong place. Drop the light, give it a cave it can enter at 14 inches, and keep the pH alkaline. They are poor jumpers compared with an arowana, and they are still large enough to displace a lid if something spooks them. Cover the tank. A 15-year-old fish on the floor is a bad end to a slow life. Buy captive-bred juveniles if you can. Wild deep-water fish take the move harder, and the lake does not need the extra pressure.",
  ],

  "fish/german-blue-ram": [
    "German blue rams are Mikrogeophagus ramirezi, a small cichlid that wants warmer, softer water than the Bolivian ram, Mikrogeophagus altispinosus. The two Mikrogeophagus species are not interchangeable. Adults are 2 to 3 inches and live 3 to 4 years. Twenty gallons is the smallest tank listed for a pair. Temperament is peaceful. Difficulty is intermediate. The short life and the water are why. A ramirezi kept at Bolivian temperatures, in hard tap water, with a casual water-change habit, often dies inside a year and gives the species a worse reputation than the fish deserves.",
    "They need heat. Keep them warmer than a general community, up in the range a ram tank is known for, commonly the low to mid 80s, and keep the water soft and acidic rather than hard and alkaline. A Bolivian ram tolerates a cooler, more ordinary tank. A German blue ram in that tank fades, clamps, and wastes. The body differences are visible once you have seen both: ramirezi is smaller and more delicately marked, with a longer point on the dorsal, and the electric-blue and gold forms are color lines of this species, not of the Bolivian. Those color lines are usually more fragile than a wild-type ramirezi, not less.",
    "A 20-gallon tank holds a pair if the rest of the stocking is light. Sand, a flat stone or a broad leaf, and plants that break the tank into rooms match the way they spawn. Both parents tend the eggs. They are peaceful with midwater tetras and with corydoras until the spawn, and then they chase anything off the site. The chase is brief and local in a tank with room. In a crowded 20-gallon tank the chased fish has nowhere to sit. Shrimp are a poor bet. A ram will pick them off when it is hunting food for fry, and sometimes before.",
    "Feed small foods often. A micro pellet, frozen daphnia, and baby brine shrimp keep a 2-inch cichlid in condition better than one large meal of bloodworms. They are slow eaters next to danios. If the tank is busy, feed the rams in their corner. A sunken belly on a German blue ram is an emergency for that fish even when the tetras look fat. Three to 4 years is the whole life. A fish that spends the first six months underfed has already lost a chunk of it.",
    "Intermediate care is the temperature stability and the water quality, not a complicated social scheme. They do not uproot the tank like a convict and they do not need a group of eight. They do need water that stays warm and clean, with nitrate kept low and no sudden cold water changes. A large cold change is a reliable way to bring on ich and to stop a pair from eating. Heat the new water. Match it. Then change it.",
    "Buy fish that are already colored and already eating. A pale ram in a shop tank at 76°F has been sitting in the wrong water, and the 3-to-4-year clock does not pause for rehabilitation. Quarantine is worth it because the species is touchy and because a new ram can bring ich into a warm tank where it spreads quickly. Do not treat them as a beginner substitute for a Bolivian ram. The Bolivian is the tougher, cooler-water fish. This one is the fish you keep when you can hold a warm, soft, stable 20-gallon tank and you accept a short life.",
  ],

  "fish/ghost-shrimp": [
    "Ghost shrimp in the pet trade are usually Palaemonetes, often Palaemonetes paludosus, a small freshwater shrimp from North America. They are not the marine cleaner shrimp and they are not an Amano. Adults reach about 1.5 to 2 inches and live 1 to 1.5 years. The card allows a tank as small as 5 gallons. They want 70 to 80°F, pH 7.0 to 8.0, general hardness 5 to 15 dGH, and carbonate hardness 3 to 10 dKH. Care is marked very easy. The short life and the sloppy labeling are the catches.",
    "Very easy means a hardy scavenger that will eat leftover flakes, a pellet, and soft algae, and that tolerates a range of ordinary tap water. It does not mean the shrimp is a cleaner you can forget. A ghost shrimp in a bare, overfed tank becomes part of the waste problem. A ghost shrimp in a tank with hungry fish becomes food. Bettas, angelfish, and any cichlid will take them. They are sold as feeders for a reason. If you wanted a shrimp that lives out its year and a half, house it with fish that ignore a 2-inch transparent animal, or house it alone.",
    "Some animals sold as ghost shrimp are Macrobrachium, larger long-arm prawns that hunt other shrimp and small fish. Look at the arms. Palaemonetes stay modest. A ghost shrimp with heavy claws and a body already past 2 inches in the shop may be the other animal, and it will not stay peaceful in a 5-gallon tank. Ask, and assume a mixed cup from a feeder tank is a gamble. Quarantine is also how you avoid introducing the planaria, hydra, and dragonfly nymphs that ride in with feeder shrimp.",
    "Breeding is marked moderate, and the larvae need special care. A female can carry eggs. The young that hatch are not miniature adults the way cherry shrimp young are. They go through a larval stage that most display tanks do not raise. Do not buy ghosts expecting a colony. Buy the number of adults you want to watch, and expect to replace them inside a year or two. One to 1.5 years is the whole span on the card. A shrimp that lives past that is a bonus, not the plan.",
    "They molt, they are clear, and a cast shell looks like a dead shrimp until you count heads. A copper treatment meant for fish is lethal to these shrimp, and so is a neglected ammonia spike in the 5-gallon minimum, which has no room for a mistake. Very easy is fair in a stable, fish-free, or fish-safe tank with a little food and a lid, because they can climb an airline. It is not fair in a reef tank, where this species does not belong, or in a bowl of feeder water that was never cycled. The marine cleaner with the red stripe is a different page and a different salt.",
  ],

  "fish/glass-catfish": [
    "Glass catfish in the hobby are Kryptopterus vitreolus, the transparent midwater catfish from Southeast Asian streams. The card lists adult size at 4 to 5 inches, lifespan at 7 to 8 years, and a tank of at least 30 gallons. They are peaceful schooling fish and the care level is intermediate. The intermediate part is the school. A glass catfish kept alone starves or fades. The body goes opaque, the fish hides, and it stops eating while the rest of the tank looks normal.",
    "They are not bottom catfish. The body hangs in the middle of the water, angled, often with the tail down, and a healthy group moves together over open substrate and between plants. A school of six or more in a 30-gallon tank is the minimum social unit that lets them rest in the open. One fish behind the filter is a dying display. They do not become bold if you wait long enough. They become thin. Add the group on the same day, into a tank that is already planted and calm.",
    "Feed them where they swim. A small pellet, frozen brine shrimp, and daphnia in the water column reach a mouth that will not grub in the gravel the way a cory does. Fast barbs and large tetras steal that food. Glass cats are slow, deliberate feeders, and they lose. Keep them with other peaceful midwater fish that are not in a feeding frenzy, or feed after the lights drop, when the glass cats come forward and the other fish have calmed down. A sunken belly on a transparent fish is easy to see and easy to miss because people look through the animal instead of at it.",
    "They want gentle flow, subdued light, and plants that give a visual break without turning the tank into a current. A powerhead aimed through the middle of the tank exhausts them. Sharp gravel is a poor match for a fish that sometimes rests low. Sand or smooth substrate, and no fish that nips, because the fins are long and the skin is the whole appeal. Fin-nippers ruin a glass cat quickly. So do rough nets. Move them in a container of water when you can, rather than in a mesh that strips the slime.",
    "Seven to 8 years is a long life for a fish people treat as a curiosity and buy singly. The 30-gallon minimum assumes the school. A larger, longer tank is where a group of adults can hover without stacking on top of each other. They are poor tankmates for boisterous cichlids and for any fish that eats small moving things at night. The transparency is health. A milky, patched, or suddenly dark glass cat is stressed, sick, or dying. Isolate a fish that turns opaque and check the group size and the feeding before you decide it is just the light.",
    "Intermediate care is mostly social and dietary. The water itself is an ordinary soft, slightly acidic community tank, kept clean and quiet. They do not need blackwater so dark you cannot see them, and they do need enough light for you to notice a thin fish. Buy captive-bred animals when they exist in the shop, and refuse the offer of a single specimen as a centerpiece. There is no centerpiece. There is a school, or there is a fish that fades.",
  ],

  "fish/goby": [
    "Goby, on a marine label, is the family Gobiidae, not one fish. The animals in the trade run from about 1 inch to 6 inches and live 3 to 8 years, in tanks from 10 gallons up past 55, depending on the species. They need 72 to 82°F, pH 8.1 to 8.4, and specific gravity 1.020 to 1.025. Most of them are carnivores. Temperament is generally peaceful. Care runs from easy to moderate. The spread is the point. A neon goby and a sand-sifting goby do not share a care sheet just because both perch on their pelvic fins.",
    "Watchman gobies, the Cryptocentrus and related fish, pair with a pistol shrimp. The shrimp digs. The goby watches. A watchman in a tank with no shrimp and no deep sand will sit in a PVC pipe and never show you the behavior you bought it for. Give them a sand bed several inches deep and a rubble pile the shrimp can brace. Do not put two pistol-shrimp pairs on top of each other in a 10-gallon tank. The shrimp will fight, and the gobies will have no burrow left.",
    "Neon gobies and their Elacatinus relatives are the small cleaners. They pick at fish and they also need frozen foods and small pellets. They are a poor choice as the only cleaner in a tank full of ich, and they are a good choice as a small peaceful fish in a reef that already has a lid. They jump less than a firefish, and they still fit through a hole around a cable. A 10-gallon minimum can hold a neon goby and should not also hold a hawkfish that eats shrimp and small gobies.",
    "Sand-sifting gobies, Valenciennea and similar genera, eat tiny animals out of the sand. They starve on a bare glass bottom under a layer of sugar-sized sand that has no life in it, and they starve in a new tank. They also shovel sand onto corals and into overflows. A mature deep sand bed and regular frozen food, offered where the goby is actually sifting, is the combination that keeps one alive. The carnivore line on this card is doing real work. An algae wafer meant for a snail does not feed these fish.",
    "Three to 8 years covers a small cleaner that lives a shorter life and a larger watchman that can pass half a decade if the burrow stays intact. Moderate care is the sand-sifter and the shrimp pair. Easy care is a neon goby in a stable reef with a lid and a little meaty food. Read the species on the tag before you trust the family name. A goby is not a blenny, it does not scrape film algae for a living, and it should not be bought to replace a lawnmower that starved.",
  ],

  "fish/gold-barb": [
    "Gold barbs are the yellow captive form of Barbodes semifasciolatus, a fish whose wild type is green and whose home water is in China, Taiwan, and Vietnam. Adults reach about 2.5 to 3 inches and live 5 to 7 years. They want a cool 64 to 75°F, pH 6.0 to 8.0, hardness 5 to 19 dGH, and a tank of at least 20 gallons. They are peaceful schooling omnivores. The gold color is a morph. A green fish in the same tank is not a different species. It is the original one, sometimes sold as a Chinese barb or a half-banded barb.",
    "The temperature is the fact that gets missed. A heater locked at 80°F for a tropical community sits above this card. Gold barbs do well in an unheated room that stays in the upper 60s and low 70s, and they fade and shorten their lives when they are kept hot. They are not a coldwater pond fish in the goldfish sense, and they are not a discus companion. The middle of their range, around 70°F, with clean water and a school, is where the color holds.",
    "Keep six or more. A pair of gold barbs in a 20-gallon tank is a shy pair. A school uses the whole length, and the males show a bit of red in the fins when they are displaying at females rather than fighting out of boredom. They are one of the barbs that can live with peaceful tankmates. Long-finned slow fish are still a risk if the school is too small. Give them plants and open swimming room, and keep the group large enough that the display stays inside the school.",
    "Feed a flake or small pellet, a vegetable flake, and frozen daphnia or brine shrimp. They are unfussy omnivores and they will also pick at soft growth. They are not an algae crew. Nitrate under 30 ppm is the ceiling here, and a 20-gallon tank with a school and a couple of other fish reaches it if the filter is a token. Five to 7 years is a solid life for a barb and it depends on the cool water more than on any special food. A gold barb in a hot, bare tank goes pale and looks like a different, cheaper fish.",
    "Buy the school you intend to keep. Adding one new gold barb to a settled group works worse than buying them together. The wild-type green fish can join the gold ones. They will school together because they are the same species. A tiger barb should not. Different genus, different temper, and a fin-nipper besides. If the shop labeled every yellow fish a gold barb, check the markings. A rosy barb is larger, redder, and not this animal. Schubert's barb, the old trade name, is this one.",
  ],

  "fish/gouramis": [
    "Gourami is a shop-shelf name for several labyrinth fish, mostly Trichogaster and the species still sold under Colisa, from Southeast Asia. The card covers bodies from 2 to 6 inches and lifespans of 4 to 8 years, in tanks from 10 to 55 gallons depending on the species. Temperature is 74 to 82°F, pH 6.0 to 7.5, hardness 4 to 15 dGH. Diet is omnivore. The temper line admits both peaceful species and semi-aggressive ones, which is why a single care sheet keeps failing people. A honey gourami and a three-spot gourami are not the same roommate.",
    "All of them breathe air at the surface with a labyrinth organ. A gap in the cover of floating plants, and a room that is not ice-cold above a warm tank, matters for every species on this page. A fish gasping at the surface may be using its labyrinth normally, or it may be in oxygen-poor water and in trouble. Look at the gills and the neighbors. If only the gourami is up top and it swims back down calmly, that is the organ. If it hangs there with clamped fins, test the water.",
    "Dwarf gouramis, Trichogaster lalius, are the pretty 3.5-inch fish with the virus problem. Honey gouramis are a different, smaller, hardier species. Pearl gouramis get larger, carry a long feeler-like pelvic fin, and stay relatively peaceful. Three-spot gouramis and their cosby and gold color forms are the ones that turn into fin-nippers and bullies as they approach the upper end of the 2-to-6-inch range. A 10-gallon tank is a honey or a single dwarf. A three-spot belongs toward the 55-gallon end of this card, not in the dwarf's tank.",
    "Males of the small species build bubble nests and will drive females that are not ready. One male and one female, with plants, is the usual pair. Two males of a nesting species in a small tank will shred fins. Feed a pellet, flake, and frozen foods. They are slow at the surface. Danios and barbs will take the meal. Choose tankmates that eat at a similar pace, or feed the gourami in the plants. Semi-aggressive, on this card, almost always means a male of the larger species, or a nesting male in a tank with no cover.",
    "Four to 8 years covers a small honey that can live a long time and a larger gourami that often does not, because it was bought as a beginner fish and housed like a dwarf. Read the species before you trust the 10-gallon end of the tank range. The peaceful end of the temperament range is the honey and the pearl. The other end is the three-spot and a male dwarf cramped with another male. Beginner to moderate is the care line. Moderate is any gourami whose adult size you did not check, plus any dwarf from a farm that cannot talk about iridovirus.",
  ],

  "fish/green-terror": [
    "Green terrors are Andinoacara rivulatus, a large South American cichlid, not a peaceful acara and not a community fish. Adults reach 8 to 12 inches and live 12 to 15 years. House an adult in at least 75 gallons. Temperament is semi-aggressive, which understates a full-grown pair. Difficulty is intermediate. The fish on the true green terror is the one with the gold or white edge on the tail and fins, the saum. A juvenile is silver-green and does not yet show why the adult needs a tank of its own.",
    "They dig, they claim caves, and they grow into fish that bite hard. A 75-gallon tank is the floor for one adult or a young pair and it is a battleground if you add tetras, angelfish, or smaller cichlids and hope the semi-aggressive line will cover it. Stock as if the animal will be a foot long and territorial. Robust tankmates of similar size, in a tank longer than the minimum, can work if you are willing to separate them later. Shrimp, small catfish, and plants rooted in the open sand will not work. The plants come up. The shrimp get eaten.",
    "A pair that spawns becomes more aggressive, not less. They clean a rock, guard the eggs, and patrol a wider circle than they did the week before. This is the same pattern as other large cichlids and it is not a phase you can decorate away. Give them a cave that cannot be dug out from under the rockwork. Stack stone on the glass, not on sand you hope will hold. A rockfall in a 75-gallon tank is a broken panel waiting to happen. Green terrors move gravel by the mouthful.",
    "Feed a cichlid pellet and meaty frozen foods, and do not feed feeder fish as a staple. They are greedy and the bioload of a 12-inch cichlid in 75 gallons is the maintenance. Intermediate care means you can keep up with that waste and you have a plan for aggression. It does not mean a special pH ritual. Ordinary neutral to slightly acidic water suits them. The heat can sit in a normal tropical band. The difficulty is the body and the temper, not a narrow KH target.",
    "Twelve to 15 years is a long time to keep a fish that may not tolerate the tankmate you bought in year two. Buy the green terror for a species tank, not as a surprise centerpiece. Do not house them with oscars, flowerhorns, or Jack Dempseys unless the aquarium is far beyond 75 gallons and you already know how you will separate a loser. Oscar, flowerhorn, and Dempsey are the same class of problem: big, mean, and messy. One green terror, a solid cave, a filter that can take the waste, and no fish small enough to be practice prey is a coherent setup.",
    "The gold saum is the field mark people argue about in shops. If the fish is young, the edge may be faint. A mislabeled blue acara stays smaller and calmer. If the animal in front of you is already past 6 inches, thick through the head, and charging the glass, it is not an electric blue acara and it does not belong in a 30-gallon tank. They will also take a finger if you are careless during a water change near a nest. Expect a spawn if a male and a female share a cave, and decide what happens to the fry before the first eggs, not after a decade of unplanned broods.",
  ],

  "fish/hatchetfish": [
    "Hatchetfish are the surface-dwelling characins in Gasteropelecus, Carnegiella, and Thoracocharax, from Amazon basin water. Depending on the species they reach 1 to 3 inches and live 3 to 5 years. The tank minimum is 20 gallons for a school of six or more. They want 74 to 82°F, pH 5.5 to 7.0, and hardness around 2 to 10 dGH. Temperament is peaceful and schooling. Diet is carnivore in the insectivore sense. Care is moderate because they jump, they feed at the surface, and they do poorly as singles.",
    "They are surface fish. The deep chest and the wing-like pectoral fins push them out of the water after insects, and in a tank that same push puts them on the floor. They need a lid, and the lid needs to be tight around every cable. They also need floating cover. A patch of floating plants, or a piece of floating wood, gives the school somewhere to sit under so they are not hovering in the open, exposed and spooked, all day. A bare bright surface makes a hatchetfish school panic. Panic is how they hit the lid and how they refuse food.",
    "Feed at the surface. Fruit flies, small floating pellets, and frozen foods that stay up for a moment match an insectivore that does not like to dive to the gravel. Bloodworms that sink to the bottom are a corydoras meal, not a hatchetfish meal. If you keep them with fast midwater fish, the hatchets may still get the surface food if you put it on the floaters. If you keep them with fish that feed only on the bottom, do not assume the hatchets will learn to dive. They often will not, and a thin hatchet is a fish that has been missing meals in plain sight.",
    "A school of six is the minimum on the card. More is calmer. One hatchetfish hides at the back of the surface and wastes. They are peaceful toward other fish and fragile toward nippers and toward any neighbor that keeps them from the top few inches. Long-finned bettas compete for the same water and nip. A tall tank with no length gives them nowhere to run. Twenty gallons in a long footprint, with the water level an inch below the lid so a jump does not crack a fish against the glass, is the practical shape.",
    "Three to 5 years is the life when the lid stays on and the food stays at the surface. Moderate care is those two habits plus soft, slightly acidic water. They do not need a blackwater tank so dark the plants die, and they do fade in hard alkaline water with a violent current. Marble hatchets, silver hatchets, and the smaller Carnegiella are different sizes inside the 1-to-3-inch band. Check which one you bought before you decide six adults will always fit the 20-gallon minimum. A 3-inch Thoracocharax school wants the tank at the roomy end of that advice. A 1-inch marbled hatchet can live in it more comfortably, and it can still leave through a gap the size of a pencil.",
  ],

  "fish/hermit-crab": [
    "The hermit crab on this page is a marine hermit, a saltwater scavenger, not a land hermit of the Coenobita kind sold in souvenir shops. Adults in the trade are about 1 to 2 inches and live 2 to 5 years. A marine tank of 10 gallons is the smallest this card allows. Standard reef salt applies here too: 72 to 82°F, pH 8.1 to 8.4, specific gravity 1.020 to 1.025, nitrate under 10 ppm. Diet is omnivore and scavenger. Temperament is listed as peaceful. Care is beginner only if you already run a marine tank and you understand the shell problem.",
    "A marine hermit dies in a land-crab cage, and a land hermit dies in this tank. The water has to be full-strength seawater, not brackish and not fresh. A 10-gallon reef or fish tank with live rock, a lid, and stable salinity is the minimum shape. They need to be submerged. They also need shells. A hermit in a shell that is too small, or a hermit with no spare shells in the tank, will kill a snail and take that shell. Sometimes it will kill a snail it does not even need, because the snail is in the way of the search.",
    "Peaceful means they do not hunt fish. It does not mean they are safe with every invertebrate. Small hermits such as some Calcinus and Clibanarius are the usual reef janitors and still pick at snails, at tube worms, and occasionally at coral tissue if they are hungry. Larger hermits, including big Dardanus, are not a 10-gallon animal and will rearrange rock and eat what they can catch. Read the species. A crab already near 2 inches with a heavy claw may be at the top of this card's size and a poor fit for a nano reef full of astrea snails.",
    "Feed them on purpose. Algae, leftover frozen mysis, and a sinking pellet are the scavenger diet. A hermit in a spotless new tank with no film and no target feeding will go hunting. That hunt is the snail deaths people blame on a mean crab. A fed crab in a tank with several empty shells of the next size up is a much calmer animal. Replace shells as the crab grows. A decorative painted shell from a craft shop can be toxic and is often the wrong shape. Plain empty marine shells are the ones to offer.",
    "Two to 5 years is the span if salinity stays put and the crab can molt. Molting hermits bury or hide and should not be pulled out for inspection. An empty shell with no crab in it is a clue to go looking, not proof the animal died. They are sensitive to copper and to a lot of reef medications. A tank you plan to dose with copper is a tank that should not have a hermit as a permanent resident. Beginner care is a stable nano reef, a spare shell, and a little food. It is not a cup of fresh water, and it is not a dry terrarium with a sponge.",
    "Keep the number small. Several hermits in 10 gallons will fight over the best shell even if each one has a spare, and the fights end with a torn abdomen, which is fatal because the abdomen is soft. One or two small hermits, shells available, snails you are willing to risk or no snails at all, is the stocking that matches the peaceful line. If the goal is algae control, a snail that cannot be evicted is the more peaceful tool. The hermit is a scavenger with a housing problem, and the housing problem is the whole hobby of keeping it.",
  ],

  "fish/hillstream-loach": [
    "Hillstream loaches are the flattened, sucker-bodied fish from fast Asian streams, especially Sewellia and its relatives. The card files them under Balitoridae, the older family name many shops still use. Adults reach about 2 to 3 inches and live 8 to 10 years. Start with 20 gallons if the flow is actually strong. Temperament is peaceful. Difficulty is intermediate. The intermediate part is oxygen, flow, and temperature. A warm, still community tank slowly kills a fish that looks exotic and sturdy on the glass.",
    "They need high oxygen and real current. A powerhead or a river manifold, aimed along smooth rock, lets them graze and rest without being torn off the stone. Still water with a gentle hang-on-back filter is not their stream. They also need cooler water than a typical tropical mix. A heater set for a discus or a ram is too warm. Keep them in the cool side of tropical, with the surface broken so the water stays saturated. In a hot week, the loaches are the first fish to stop grazing and sit in the outflow with their gills working.",
    "The diet is biofilm. A mature tank with algae film on rounded stone is a feeding station. A brand-new tank with bare plastic rock is a fast. Blanched vegetables, a little gel food, and algae wafers can supplement the film. They are not carnivores and they are not a substitute for a corydoras. They also are not clown loaches. A hillstream loach stays small, does not click, and does not need a cave pile. It needs stone, flow, and something growing on that stone.",
    "Sharp gravel cuts them. The body is pressed to the substrate and the fins are in constant contact. Use smooth river stone and rounded gravel or sand, and skip the crushed glass and the jagged quartz. A cut hillstream loach picks up infections in the same high-flow tank that otherwise keeps it healthy. Eight to 10 years is a long life for a 3-inch fish and it happens when the skin stays intact and the water stays cool and moving. A scarred belly on a loach that will not stick to the rock is a substrate injury until you have a reason to think otherwise.",
    "They are peaceful with each other and with fish that can handle the flow. A school of six or more shows the little sparring matches over a favorite rock, which look fierce and rarely damage anything. One loach spends its life glued to one stone and never quite behaves like the species. Do not house them with fish that need still, hot, soft water. The compromise tank is wrong for both sides. A 20-gallon tank can work if the flow is strong and the rock is smooth. It cannot work as a decoration on a betta setup. Intermediate means you build the stream first and buy the loach second.",
  ],

  "fish/honey-gourami": [
    "Honey gouramis are Trichogaster chuna, a small labyrinth fish that stays about 2 inches and lives 4 to 8 years. House them from 10 gallons up. They want 72 to 82°F and pH 6.0 to 7.5. Diet is omnivore. Temperament is peaceful. Care is listed as beginner, and for once the label is closer to fair, with one condition: they are not dwarf gouramis. Dwarf gouramis commonly carry an iridovirus with no treatment. Honeys are a different species and a hardier one. Buying a honey because the dwarfs in the shop look sick is a reasonable decision.",
    "Males in breeding color are amber-gold with a dark throat and a dark belly stripe. Females are plainer and a little larger. The sunset and red morphs are color forms of the same fish. Wild-type honeys are duller and calmer in a planted tank. Two males in a 10-gallon tank will bicker. One male and one female, or a single male with peaceful roommates, fits the minimum. They are shy. A bare tank with fast danios makes a honey gourami hide under the filter and slowly thin out. Plants, a dimmer corner, and tankmates that do not win every race to the food are the setup.",
    "They breathe at the surface. Leave an opening in any floating cover and keep a lid on the tank. They are poor jumpers compared with a hatchetfish and they still find a gap. Feed a small pellet, flake, and frozen daphnia. They pick food out of the plants and from the surface. They lose to barbs and to zebra danios. A 10-gallon tank of honey gouramis and chili rasboras or ember tetras is a better match than a honey in a barb school. The peaceful label assumes the neighbors are peaceful too.",
    "Bubble nests happen. A male under a leaf, spitting bubbles, is ready to spawn and will chase the female for a while. In a planted 10-gallon tank she can get away. In an empty tank she cannot. Fry are tiny and most community tanks eat them. Four to 8 years is a long life for a 2-inch fish and it is realistic when the water stays in range and nobody is nipping the feelers. Those pelvic filaments are easy to damage. A honey with shortened, white-tipped feelers is being chewed on, even if you have not seen it happen.",
    "Beginner care here means a planted 10-gallon tank, a filter with a gentle outflow, and no medication habits borrowed from a fish tank that uses copper or a harsh dye every time a fin looks ragged. They are sensitive to dirty water and to sudden cold more than they are sensitive to a pH that sits calmly at 7. Keep ammonia and nitrite at zero and do not park the heater at the top of the range for no reason. A honey gourami is the small peaceful gourami to choose when a dwarf gourami is the fish the shop is pushing and the shop cannot explain the virus.",
  ],

  "fish/jack-dempsey": [
    "Jack Dempseys are Rocio octofasciata, a Central American cichlid named for a boxer and built like the name stuck. Adults reach 10 to 15 inches and live 10 to 15 years. One fish needs at least 55 gallons. A pair needs 75 gallons or more. Temperature is a wide 72 to 86°F, pH 6.5 to 7.5, hardness about 8 to 12 dGH. The menu runs from pellets to meaty frozen food. Temperament is aggressive. Care is intermediate. They are not a community fish, and the electric-blue color form is still a Jack Dempsey.",
    "A juvenile is blue-spangled and small enough to look like a candidate for a tetra tank. It will not stay that size, and it will not keep that manners. By the time the fish is half grown it is digging pits, charging anything that enters its half of the tank, and eating any roommate that fits in its mouth. Stock the 55-gallon minimum as a species tank for one fish, or step up to 75 gallons or more before you try a pair. A pair that has not spawned can look compatible. A pair with eggs often cannot be housed with anything else at all.",
    "They dig. Plants in the open substrate come out. Rockwork has to sit on the bottom glass or it will be undermined. A cave is optional and will be remodeled. Leave heaters and filter intakes where a 15-inch fish cannot slam them. The bioload matches the appetite. A carnivore pellet, frozen seafood, and earthworms are plenty. Feeder fish are a parasite delivery system and a bad habit. Nitrate under 30 ppm on this card still takes water changes once the fish is eating like an adult. A 55-gallon tank with a small filter will not stay there.",
    "Aggressive means plan on it, not hope it will be the mild one. Some individuals tolerate a bristlenose or a large, fast dither fish for a while. Many do not. Do not add angelfish, discus, small South American cichlids, or anything you are fond of. Do not add them to a tank with an oscar, a green terror, or a flowerhorn unless the tank is far larger than 75 gallons and you have already decided how to separate the loser. An oscar, a green terror, and a flowerhorn are tank-busters on their own. Putting the greatest hits in one aquarium is not a stocking plan.",
    "The electric-blue Jack Dempsey is a color line of the same species. It is not calmer, it is not smaller, and it still needs 55 gallons for one fish. If anything, a thin blue juvenile from a high-volume hatchery needs cleaner water and a more careful diet than a wild-type fish that arrived already sturdy. Ten to 15 years is the life if the tank grows with the fish. Intermediate care is the size and the temper. The pH band is ordinary. People get into trouble because the juvenile looked easy, not because the chemistry was obscure.",
    "A Dempsey that glass-surfs all day in a bare 55-gallon tank is bored and cramped, not showing off. Give it length, a few solid objects, and a feeding routine, and keep the lid on. They do not jump like an arowana and they do displace covers when they bolt. Fifteen inches of cichlid on the carpet is a grim surprise. Buy the fish when the tank is already running at the size on this card. Upgrading later, with an adult Dempsey in a holding tub, is the hard way to learn a number that was printed before you brought it home.",
  ],
};
